window.harnessChunks["rows-7001d95f272aee6c.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T12:53:31Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/maximarhipkin/ferrule\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ferrule</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/maximarhipkin/ferrule/tree/24023deffa325405db3f260a5a03d736976fa1b0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 24023deffa325405db3f260a5a03d736976fa1b0\">24023de &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute coding/assistant/scheduled outcomes through a model/tool loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific files, commands, service responses, tool failures and verification errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select actions, tool arguments and continuation after observations. - Decision owner: root and child model-backed agents within their assigned outcomes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, tool registry, sandbox/credential proxy, verification, transcript, scheduler and budget/stuck guards. - Closure path: call → tools → observations → repeated model choice; returned results and failed check tails enter subsequent operation. - Boundary reachability: shipped run/chat/gateway and child factories construct ferrulecore::Agent and equip product tools; no external cognitive harness is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: enforcement alone cannot choose the next task-dependent action if the model is removed. - Evidence: shipped runtime; core loop entry; action/verification loop; child construction. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-relative finding; no empirical model-quality claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent duplicate task effort and mutually destructive sibling workspace edits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two children attempt the same work or modify the same parent checkout. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose/claim a non-conflicting task, select worktree isolation and decide when to integrate a returned branch. - Decision owner: participating model agents, with the root owning integration into its checkout. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: atomic SQLite claims/dependency refusals, isolated worktrees and returned branch/workspace notes. - Closure path: claim refusal returns current ownership/dependency information; agents select other work or wait; worker changes remain isolated until root merge/cherry-pick. - Boundary reachability: the supplied tasks and spawnagent tools are registered for the actual running tree, not hypothetical external coordination code. - Distinct S1 units: concurrent root/worker/planner agents producing separate code/work outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: duplicate task claims and concurrent code edits contaminating one another. - Attenuating coordination relation: exclusive task claim plus per-worker worktree and root-controlled integration. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: claim/result/branch metadata changes what work an agent can take and which changes enter subsequent parent operation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: atomic task ownership and workspace separation directly regulate identified duplicate-effort/edit collisions; the shared board alone is not the witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents choose tasks/isolation and root integration; transactions/git merely enforce those choices. - Evidence: task choice/feedback contract; atomic conflict-aware claim; tool dispatch; isolation wiring; worktree integration boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Outside git or on worktree failure a child may share the workspace; this is reported, not claimed equally isolated.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current child commitments/capacity and deployment spending on behalf of the root tree. - Disturbance / variety regulated: too many live commitments, shared token scarcity and children needing intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: root selects wait/message/stop and renewed child effort; parent selects shared caps and approved administrative intervention. - Decision owner: root agent in the base tree-control mode; legitimate self-hosting operator in the separate parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child status/token projection, budget warnings, stop flags/tree accounting, admin approval cards, shared Hub caps and audit/config persistence. - Closure path: root interventions change running child behaviour or stop descendants; approved parent cap changes are applied to the live Hub and subsequent runs are governed by them. - Boundary reachability: messageagent/stopagent are actual root tools; owner chat ferruleadmin and dashboard use the same first-party control APIs. No adjacent benchmark supervisor is credited. - Whole-system current view: root lists its current children with role/status/token use and reports, and receives tree budget feedback; operator sees gateway health/current turns, tasks and shared caps. - Current-control decision scope: current operational commitments and scarce tree/deployment resources, beyond merely decomposing the user&#39;s task. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the root chooses intervention under current operational feedback; fixed max-depth/token checks do not own that choice. In parent mode the operator&#39;s exact approval/cap decision owns the intervention. - Evidence: current child view and intervention tools; current consumption view; tree budget feedback; parent health/current control; operator status view; live cap return; approval execution. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A is scoped to root-tree current commitments; it is not an autonomous governor of all gateway roots or OSS governance. The P mode is independently supported, not simultaneous dual ownership.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: optionally challenge a worker/parent claim or code change through a separate verifier&#39;s raw-artifact access. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a plausible completion report may hide broken tests, incorrect code or contaminated evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: verifier chooses proof commands/code inspection and judges whether the submitted claim holds. - Decision owner: a non-forked verifier agent in a separate session; parent acts on returned findings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verifier-specific prompt/role, throwaway workspace snapshot, separate transcript, optional distinct provider and parent notice/report return. - Closure path: verifier result is delivered to the parent, which can reject the claim, redirect/restart work or inspect further through its existing message/stop/tool paths. - Boundary reachability: spawnagent role=verifier is a shipped runtime mode with role-specific placement and child construction, not a test/eval-only role. - Claim being audited: a submitted operational completion claim or code change. - Ordinary reporting path: worker final report/status to parent. - Complementary access path: verifier directly reads the copied code including uncommitted/new files and runs proof commands. - Independence boundary: separate non-forked model context and snapshot; verifier does not implement/repair production changes. Same provider is allowed but is not claimed as model independence. - Who acts on findings: the root/parent with authority to redirect or stop the relevant child and change its own next operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verifier model owns the evidence-selection/verdict, rather than a deterministic parser or self-reported success flag. - Evidence: audit role purpose; snapshot placement; fresh child context/role; return and parent response modes; verifier workspace boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Optional claim-relative audit mode, not every verifier invocation. Forking producer context, using it as ordinary same-path QA, or choosing non-isolated shared work can invalidate this independence claim; no live audit or general reliability guarantee is asserted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supplied outside-and-future adaptation process with the required supervisory coupling established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future external provider/tool/user changes could demand capability adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped future-relevant option judgment and two-way S3 adoption loop established. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: offline reflection/probing/playbook deltas, model discovery/testing/fallbacks, doctor and install/update/admin operations. - Closure path: learning applies lessons to later prompts and admin changes current capability; the inspected triggers are retrospective internal failures or present configuration needs, without an explicit external/prospective adaptation distinction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: learning between runs and autonomous self-configuration are not sufficient S4 evidence. - Evidence: retrospective learning inputs; learning implementation; contrastive pass scope; admin change scope. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Source-relative finding; no empirical model-quality claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: retain legitimate ultimate authority over the deployment&#39;s autonomy/security envelope. - Disturbance / variety regulated: whether Ferrule may act without per-change consent while protected keys and owner-only boundaries remain reserved. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the operator chooses Ask me first versus Protected autonomous and controls security settings that the bot cannot alter. - Decision owner: the authenticated deployment owner via the dashboard; the model cannot grant itself this authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: page-only sandbox-mode API, confirmation/fresh-config guard, protected configuration keys, audit record, saved policy and gateway restart. - Closure path: the owner&#39;s mode choice is saved and audited; the gateway restarts with the returned sandbox/trust envelope, changing subsequent admin/tool behaviour. - Boundary reachability: dashboard sandboxmode is a first-party operating endpoint explicitly omitted from chat operation routing; owner-mode and supervised admin prompts/tools are wired alternatives. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: the system&#39;s standing autonomy/delegation and protected-secret authority boundary, rather than approval of a single task. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: deployment owner; autonomous admin operation inside the envelope does not authorize changing the envelope itself. - Return-to-operation path: confirmed owner choice → policy/config save → gateway restart → subsequent operation under chosen consent/autonomy mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model may perform permitted admin work, but cannot decide its own ultimate sandbox/delegation policy. - Evidence: owner-only envelope and protected limits; actual authoritative switch and return; distinct subsequent admin behaviour; chat routes omit sandbox authority. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Linux owner mode has platform-specific constraints. This is deployment-parent policy, not a claim of project-level governance or that ordinary approval cards alone are S5.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ea30365da43e2bcde1d7bcb779bed890626be1d6/assessments/fe/ferrule.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ea30365da43e2bcde1d7bcb779bed890626be1d6\">ea30365 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T21:13:02+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/davesheffer/coding-orchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Coding Orchestrator</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/davesheffer/coding-orchestrator/tree/dca682d7a1bc99c6b83508ad03186b2015085e8a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dca682d7a1bc99c6b83508ad03186b2015085e8a\">dca682d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomous coding/research/test work by the host-model agent and installed native scout/runner/builder agents with bounded task/tool loops. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain repository structure, source changes, failing checks, implementation alternatives and task-local tool feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the main session model designs/solves ambiguous work, the scout selects what to investigate within its brief, the builder chooses concrete edits/validation for a specified change, and the runner interprets exact check outcome without changing the task&#39;s scope. - Decision owner: Claude Code/Codex main-session and named-child models reachable through installed first-party role contracts, not the Python installer or preconfigured model strings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: installed role files, native subagent invocation, explicit model/effort/brief, read-only helper/guard, sandbox checks where supported, typed outcome report and optional Windows launcher. - Closure path: user objective → main model investigation/design and bounded role assignment → child-model tools/read/check/edit feedback → RESULT/EVIDENCE/CONFIDENCE/UNVERIFIED → main model checks, corrects or completes task. - Boundary reachability: default installers put the role definitions and main charter into the selected assistant home; starting a supported Claude/Codex session exposes the named roles. The Windows launcher separately supplies a real restricted CLI fallback, while native host execution is conditional on role discovery and effective permissions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves static roles, installers, command/report helpers and sandbox policy but no task-specific decisions or coding output. The external client executes inference, while the installed first-party definitions directly shape real normal-operation model roles. - Evidence: role dispatch rules, Claude role routing, builder role, scout role, launch restrictions. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for distributed role construction; provider-authenticated live role selection and effective sandbox enforcement were not run here. - Caveats: an optional Windows launcher cannot be silently assumed to work on Linux/macOS, and mere role model metadata is weaker than observing a live session. The role pack&#39;s own validation record describes these native gaps.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: direct attenuation of overlapping file edits and order-dependent interference among several independent coding S1 subunits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent builders/writers touching one file or repository without disjoint ownership, overlapping edits and approvals during unresolved prior work, and invalid test results produced before a dependency completes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the main model assigns disjoint paths and bounded briefs to independent subagents, keeps conflicting edits/dependencies sequential, and changes assignment/wait order after builder diffs, check exits and verification indicate overlap or dependency failure. - Decision owner: installed main-session orchestration model, constrained by real user scope and native permission boundaries; deterministic role safety guards are not the coordination decision maker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role separation (scout/critic read-only, runner check-only, builder scoped writing), explicit file-ownership clauses, dependency/wait rules, actual diffs and result reports, host&#39;s task completion notices, and conditional checked sandbox boundaries. - Closure path: proposed independent work may touch the same mutable code or require an earlier fix → main model gives non-overlapping ownership or delays the dependent worker → observed diff/check/report returns → main model corrects assignment or waits/replans → later model-owned S1 edits use the revised scope/order. - Boundary reachability: both installed main-session charters explicitly instruct disjoint file ownership, parallelism only for independent units, sequential edits/dependencies and checking actual child results; native named agents are reachable after installation. No unrelated external work manager is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main model decides which changes are independent and how to respond to observed overlap; static role restrictions and a guard may reduce the chance of unsafe writes but cannot select a domain-specific collision resolution. - Distinct S1 units: at least two concurrently or sequentially handed-off native child models (for example separate scout/runner and builder branches, or two scoped builders) working on one coding project under the same main model. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two builders can produce conflicting edits to shared files, while a runner checking an incomplete dependency can report stale results that mislead the later builder/parent. - Attenuating coordination relation: first-party parent role contract with disjoint file ownership, specified briefs and ordered dependency/editor actions, plus returned diffs/reports and correction by the main model. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the main model inspects observed builder diffs and real runner exits, then narrows or revises a worker&#39;s file scope, reassigns the fix or delays the next writer until conflicting work is reconciled. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: disjoint ownership and dependent-write/check ordering are aimed at a concrete interference among independently acting code-changing S1 roles, not merely routing routine low-cost tasks. - Evidence: Codex no-overlap/task/dependency contract, Claude task partition, builder scoped writing, Codex named role definition. - Basis: explicit skill-driven inter-writer operating policy. - Confidence: medium for model-owned S2 in correctly followed multi-role operation; no runtime global file-claim database or automatic source-collision detector exists. - Caveats: generic task delegation or read-only scout/critic isolation alone is not S2; this claim rests on distinct changing/observing S1 units and source-overlap prevention/correction through the parent model. A single writer mode does not exercise inter-S1 coordination.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: whole-current project control by the main model over delegated task commitments, uncertainty, verification obligations and risk/acceptance interventions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing current tasks, unverified work, weak reports, elapsed context, child sandbox failures, failed tests, risk/irreversible actions and incomplete handoffs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: main model chooses whether to delegate or handle directly, allocates roles/briefs, analyzes child reports/diffs and risk, requires another check/critic, changes task plan/assignment and accepts or returns work for correction. - Decision owner: installed main-session orchestration model. A real user retains authorization over pushes and irreversible actions, but no distinct parent-governed whole-current S3 mode is inferred from occasional permission approval. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RESULT/EVIDENCE/CONFIDENCE/UNVERIFIED reports, role constraints, verification commands, client child-task state, optional Mission Control in-flight UI, agent-report.py, relay context gauge/handoff, Jev route/guard hints and actual client permission enforcement. - Closure path: current parallel child outcomes/changed files, low-confidence reports and check exits → model main-session portfolio judgment → correction/delegation/escalation/critic/pass decision → revised child work, accepted code or bounded new session continuation. - Boundary reachability: the main-session charter is installed and loaded as the normal operating instruction; native role responses return to that session, optional UI/launcher provide further observations. Its job is explicitly design, judgment, verification and final response rather than merely waiting for a model child to finish. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static rules and context thresholds advise or constrain; the host model owns what to do with the whole current request and whether evidence is sufficient. A dashboard, hook or permission check does not exercise that judgment alone. - Whole-system current view: main conversation receives each launched child result and task status, and can inspect diffs/checks; optional Mission Control displays the active model/agent timeline and report cards. The optional Windows launcher aggregates its own bounded run reports, not arbitrary independent client sessions. - Current-control decision scope: split/reassign project work, sequence disjoint edits, require direct validation or critic review, stop an unsafe course, and choose release readiness within user-granted authority. - Evidence: Codex parent-owned supervision, Claude parent-owned supervision, Mission Control current observation, agent report helper, role handoff contracts. - Basis: explicit operating ownership, supported by native role/report interfaces. - Confidence: medium-high for supported agent-caller mode. - Caveats: Claude shell restrictions may be merely advisory under inherited permissions; actual client permissions and model availability must be verified on deployment. The Mission Control mod is optional and observability, not an independent controller.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: model-owned independent challenge of risky code changes and correctness/security claims using direct source and verification evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementer&#39;s unsupported confidence, a passing-but-irrelevant test, concurrency/security bugs missed by builder and main agent, and accidental self-review of an asserted fix. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separately instantiated critic model with a fresh brief independently judges the exact diff/source and produces SHIP | FIX FIRST | RETHINK findings; the main model then resolves/acts on the evidence before claiming the risky work done. - Decision owner: shipped critic role running as a distinct Claude/Codex model agent for independent audit judgment; main model owns remediation and final acceptance, rather than the builder or an automatic CI lint gate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh-context adversarial role prompts, read-only policy (with effective-enforcement caveats), exact diff/base and claim, expanding through callers/error paths/tests, failure scenarios, line-anchored findings, typed verdict and main-session correction loop. - Closure path: builder/main proposes risky change or completion claim → separately briefed critic reads direct changed code and adjacent paths → independent finding/SHIP/FIX FIRST/RETHINK returns → main model verifies, requests changes or documents conscious remaining risk before acceptance. - Boundary reachability: Claude installer places agents/critic.md, Codex installer places the native critic.toml; both installed main charters explicitly require critic review for risky or irreversible work. It does not rely on a repo maintainer PR process or a hypothetical external assessor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the critic model owns a substantive adversarial audit verdict from complementary direct evidence; the static role manifest/guards and main model&#39;s acceptance are separate functions. The builder&#39;s own test/report is not substituted for an independent critic. - Claim being audited: that a proposed coding change or high-risk operation is correct, safe and supported by the stated check evidence. - Ordinary reporting path: builder or runner returns RESULT/EVIDENCE/CONFIDENCE/UNVERIFIED and main reads the actual work/checks. - Complementary access path: distinct fresh-context critic receives the exact diff/base and claim, reads actual code/callers/error cases/tests without accepting author reasoning, and reports concrete adverse scenarios with file/line evidence. - Independence boundary: critic has separate role, model/prompt/brief and read-only posture, no shared author conclusions as settled truth; practical sandbox isolation depends on actual client/host and may be unverified even when role files state read-only. - Who acts on findings: the main orchestration model investigates blocker/major/minor evidence, orders corrective work, reruns checks or explicitly withholds final acceptance; an automatic critic response does not publish changes. - Evidence: Claude critic charter, Codex critic definition, fresh critic routing/feedback, Claude critic acceptance, native validation boundaries. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for distinct installed critical review path, subject to client runtime configuration. - Caveats: runtime enforcement for Claude critic may remain instruction-level under permissive inherited modes, and no paid critic ran in this assessment. Optional user refusal or unsupported native role availability may prevent a specific deployment from exercising S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no installed organizational future-environment sensing → adaptation-option generation → decision → returned operating capability loop is supplied for the assessed host coding project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model tiers, provider availability, token use and future tooling change appear as configuration/routing concerns, not a functionally closed S4 strategy system. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S4 owner is bound to a proactive future adaptation cycle; agent choices over current work and context rollover are S3/continuity decisions. - Decision owner: neither autonomous system-model intelligence authority nor a qualifying distinct parent-governed S4 mode is established within the installed bundle. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model-routing table and fallback rules, context gauge/rollover, optional Jev classification, agent-report.py, benchmarks/optimization research and human configuration updates. - Closure path: no evidenced future/external distinction → novel adaptation option → authoritative selection → returned change to current system capability. A context threshold may trigger a fresh session but preserves current objectives instead of adapting future organizational capabilities. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the host model can choose a different helper or consult benchmarks, but the released bundle does not supply a strategic outside-and-then intelligence role and completed adaptation return loop. - Evidence: role/routing and continuity rules, Claude relay instructions, relay code, Jev routing, benchmarks. - Basis: bounded first-party absence. - Confidence: medium-high for examined installation and runtime paths. - Caveats: separate external corporate/user strategy may change the role bundle; the repo&#39;s benchmark and optimization materials are not an installed, agent-owned prospective capability governance loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party autonomous or parent-governed ultimate identity/policy decision and subsequent charter-return loop is installed for the coding system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task scope, authorization, tool/network boundaries, project requirements and model availability are externally configured and handled as task/permission governance, not identity disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user grants or withholds outward/destructive actions and any optional permission fallback; main agent respects the configured role policy, but cannot adjudicate its own ultimate identity or bind the user/project through an S5 resolution institution. - Decision owner: no legitimate S5 mode owner within the installed runtime is demonstrated. User-owned config/approval remains at an external parent but is not a full S5 function-specific return path merely because permission checks exist. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: installed AGENTS/CLAUDE instructions, role sandbox settings, user-owned agent-routing.json exception list, external client permissions, Jev risk gate and optional Hunch outside the actual installed managed block. - Closure path: blocked tool or permission fallback request → user/current-model task decision → resumed/denied particular operation; no defined ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authoritative verdict → binding identity charter update → subsequent operation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent following constraints has S1/S3 operating rights but no supreme authority to change the system&#39;s charter. A user can reinstall/change settings, but installing code and occasional approval are not S5 parent governance. - Evidence: Codex role permission and consent boundaries, user-local policy handling, Claude parent authority limits, installed managed-block boundary, opt-in Jev guard. - Basis: bounded first-party absence. - Confidence: high for no shipped identity-level return forum at declared boundary. - Caveats: independent OSS users may each govern their assistant installations; do not aggregate their private settings into an organization-wide S5 or borrow the separate Hunch system as this bundle&#39;s own installed authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/20404580837d682e46e9d185d4f7cbf08fdb4888/assessments/co/coding-orchestrator.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 20404580837d682e46e9d185d4f7cbf08fdb4888\">2040458 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T21:04:28+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CodeAlive-AI/pragmatic-orchestration\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pragmatic Orchestration</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CodeAlive-AI/pragmatic-orchestration/tree/cc829d908822978fe7fce4670967525ea3a9a267\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cc829d908822978fe7fce4670967525ea3a9a267\">cc829d9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: independent model-directed coding, codebase research and validation inside a bounded delegated task, including normal host-model task work when no external worker is needed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing source and requirements, implementation choices, test/tool feedback and partial failures within one assigned workspace and task contract. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the selected external coding-agent model chooses investigation, edits, tools, tests and substantive answer inside its assigned scope; the caller model chooses the task contract and later acceptance but does not precompute every worker action. - Decision owner: configured Codex/Claude/OpenCode/Grok/Devin model actor in each actual delegated CLI session; a supported skill-capable host model owns its own nondelegated task decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: porch delegate agent selection, backend adapters, configured model/profile, prompt transport, exact cwd, session/result artifacts, supervisor/registry, bounded status and final-result delivery; external provider models supply inference rather than Porch-owned hidden capabilities. - Closure path: host model assigns a bounded objective → backend model executes its own task/tool feedback loop → code/research/check result and evidence return through the supervisor → calling model checks/accepts or steers/reassigns unfinished work. - Boundary reachability: SKILL.md routes installed skill-capable callers to the shipped executable porch entrypoint, whose delegate dispatcher, Python supervisor and adapters genuinely start the configured worker; this is an advertised real use path, not a test-only fake agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without a model-driven worker, the wrapper can transport tasks and persist events but cannot select the task-specific actions or produce substantive code; without the caller model, the skill does not itself choose the task contract. - Evidence: installed skill delegation map, delegate launcher, supervisor and adapter invocation, delegate runtime. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high for shipped model/task/runtime connection; no live provider invocation was attempted. - Caveats: the actual provider binary, account/quota and selected skill-capable host are external installation dependencies. Do not infer provider-internal multi-agent coordination as Porch S2/S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuation of conflicts between independently writing S1 agents during overlapping project delegation and corrective replacement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent writes to the same mutable checkout, a successor modifying partially written files before its predecessor is actually stopped, and different task scopes modifying each other&#39;s work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model caller gives disjoint task scopes and uses distinct user-authorized workspaces for concurrent writers; when new events/diffs expose conflict or failure, it decides whether to redirect/stop an exact worker, preserve partial changes, or withhold a replacement assignment until the writer is confirmed stopped. - Decision owner: the skill-guided calling model in the supported multi-delegate mode. The user has prior authority over workspace creation; the supervisor/registry only enforces run-addressed lifecycle and transport. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate user-provided working directories, explicit task scope contracts, run IDs, delegate list/events/status/cancel/steer, stop/acknowledgment verification, VCS status and diff checks, and the first-party overlapping-writer guard in delegation policy. - Closure path: two model worker tasks could contend for one checkout → model caller assigns distinct approved workspaces or keeps the replacement waiting → observes run outcomes/partial diffs → sends corrective steer or cancels and confirms terminal status → only then permits the next conflicting S1 writer to act. - Boundary reachability: the shipped skill has explicit multi-worker/parallel-writer and stop-before-reassignment instructions while porch exposes independent running worker IDs, separate cwd binding, observation and targeted intervention. This is a supported optional multiple-delegate operating arrangement, conditional on user-authorized workspaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the caller model judges actual overlap and which worker/scope to change; the private registry prevents mis-addressed control messages but does not detect or autonomously resolve file conflicts. - Distinct S1 units: at least two independently executing coding agents each with a separate model/tool loop and one assigned project task/working directory. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous unrelated writers on one checkout can overwrite, intermix or invalidate changes; premature reassignment into a predecessor&#39;s partially modified tree repeats the conflict. - Attenuating coordination relation: first-party mandatory separate-workspace/clear-scope policy plus substantive parent checkpointing, explicit stop/verify before conflicting replacement, and corrective steering into subsequent tasks. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: run event/cancellation outcome and inspected repository diff go to the model caller; it changes the next worker&#39;s root/scope, or sends the exact active worker a corrective task before continuing. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the skill identifies a concrete inter-writer contention hazard and prescribes an operational anti-overlap/feedback relation among distinct worker loops, not merely a mailbox or job queue. - Evidence: delegate multi-writer and replacement contract, parent skill contract, run-addressed control commands, remote workspace guidance. - Basis: explicit operating contract backed by active run-addressed tools. - Confidence: medium: model-owned preventive and corrective protocol is shipped, but there is no automatic cross-workspace write-collision detector or proof that callers always follow the skill. - Caveats: single-delegate or manually co-located writers do not realize this S2 path. Runtime locks protect metadata/mailboxes, not shared repository writes; user must authorize separate writer workspaces.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: model-caller regulation of the whole current delegated project workload and commitments through project supervision, intervention and acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled worker progress, wrong task interpretations, partial or failed outcomes, unexpected deviations, competing active work, uncertainty over delivery/steering and incomplete acceptance criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the calling model evaluates the active delegation portfolio and substantive evidence, chooses new assignments/reprioritization, corrective steering, cancellation, reassignment and whether work is accepted or requires further verification. - Decision owner: the operating skill-capable host model in its agent-orchestrator mode; CLI lifecycle commands are instruments and user scope constraints remain binding. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: current run enumeration, events cursors, wait-any group snapshots, per-run complete final texts, exact steer delivery status, deviation journals, verified tests/diffs and optional remote-host status. - Closure path: several active worker status/progress/evidence streams → host model forms current project-wide judgment → targeted steer, cancel, replacement, revised workload or acceptance → subsequent worker execution/commitments and verification change. - Boundary reachability: the installed skill expressly directs the model caller to inspect each worker during its first minute, adapt later supervision, keep the parent turn active, observe several runs and make intervention/acceptance decisions; all named control/observation commands are routed to shipped CLI and supervisor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model discretion determines current priorities, whether the work is useful, scope of corrective action and acceptance. A process exit, heartbeat, quota readout or wait-any timeout alone would not make that judgment. - Whole-system current view: delegate list --active enumerates the private run registry, while wait-any reports complete supplied-group state/timing and events/artifacts provide substantive per-worker evidence; the calling model must retain the project group membership rather than infer it from a global list. - Current-control decision scope: allocate distinct worker contracts, redirect exact active sessions, stop unproductive work, delay conflicting replacement, coordinate completion and accept/reject worker contributions against task criteria. - Evidence: model caller supervision and acceptance contract, group wait and event interfaces, registry control, parent-owned review acceptance. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for model-run supported project supervision, absent end-to-end provider session observation. - Caveats: neither the CLI nor optional read-only UI has autonomous whole-fleet scheduling, and a human issuing an occasional CLI stop is not enough to establish a separate operational parent-governed S3 mode. Periodic checks are a caller obligation, not a built-in timer.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independent, direct-evidence auditing of implementation/correctness/security claims from worker work, with corrective results fed to the caller. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false completion reports, unsafe patches, stale or invented code quotations, defects not visible in a worker&#39;s self-summary, and one actor rubber-stamping its own work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separately launched read-only reviewer model assesses the actual source/diff and reports independently reasoned defects; optionally an independent judge model adjudicates combined discoveries, while the calling model chooses corrective disposition. - Decision owner: one or several review-mode model agents operating under the first-party audit prompt rather than the implementing worker&#39;s instruction/authority, followed by the caller model for remedial action. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: review code direct file/diff input, role-specific security/correctness and optional specialist passes, read-only review contract, per-pass independent reports, cross-check of quoted code against files, optional deterministic dedup and separate LLM judge, final report delivery. - Closure path: worker claims a change is correct/ready → independent review model directly inspects the file/diff and broadens relevant source context → source-anchored findings reach the caller → caller verifies and commands corrective work, targeted tests, or reasoned acceptance before completion. - Boundary reachability: porch review code basic is an ordinary shipped CLI mode, and review ask/super/ultra are explicit supported modes. The skill instructs the host caller to invoke reviewers separately, supply task contracts, and act on findings; no private maintainer CI reviewer is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct reviewing model agents make risk/finding judgments from independent direct access; quote checking/dedup scripts only validate or combine evidence, while the primary worker cannot rewrite those review judgments through its own completion message. - Claim being audited: a delegated coding agent&#39;s implementation/report or the caller&#39;s proposed code change satisfies task and security/correctness requirements. - Ordinary reporting path: implementing worker&#39;s session events, final answer, own tests and source changes return to its calling model through delegate wait. - Complementary access path: independent review code reader receives current source file/diff plus an explicitly incomplete seed and is required to expand its own repository search; findings carry file/line/quoted-code anchors checked against source. - Independence boundary: reviewers are separately invoked read-only model sessions with dedicated audit roles and denied edits/delegation; they are not the working delegate&#39;s self-attestation. Sharing vendor/model family or an agent-selected initial file seed does not itself guarantee diversity; optional judge is another audit pass, not substitute evidence. - Who acts on findings: the calling orchestration model verifies dispositions against the task contract and steers/assigns corrections; neither basic/specialist report nor automatic judge verdict unilaterally accepts worker changes. - Evidence: review operational mode, specialist review dispatch, quote validation, review workflow stages, task-relative remediation. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for operational independence and direct-source audit; no live reviewers were paid/run at the frozen source. - Caveats: optional review must actually be invoked for a particular task; basic/specialists do not have an internal judge, and no automated merge gate forces a review finding to block acceptance. Read-only enforcement varies by backend.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supported first-party organizational loop that senses changing external/future conditions, develops strategic alternatives and returns a capability adaptation decision into the whole current system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential changes in future provider quality, coding technology, cost, environment or long-horizon deployment capability are discussed, but not closed through a shipped prospective sensing/adaptation process. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not supplied for S4; a caller can manually choose another model/provider or alter configuration, and retrospective session history can inform ad hoc choices, without a distinct future-oriented intelligence owner and returned capability-change loop. - Decision owner: no autonomous or legitimate parent S4 decision owner is established within this distribution boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider/profile configuration, quota snapshots, session history stats/flags, advice to periodically inspect agent effectiveness, external-model comparison tips and remote-host provisioning helpers. - Closure path: no demonstrated outside-and-then sensing → alternative-generation → adaptation decision → changed future capability/S3 closure; ordinary task replanning and same-run steering are S3, not S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an optional model&#39;s analysis of past sessions could be extended into strategy, but the installed skill does not bind that analysis to first-party prospective adaptation authority and returned operating-capability change. - Evidence: skill review/delegation modes, session-history tools, configuration and quota, remote-host options. - Basis: bounded first-party absence. - Confidence: medium-high within current shipped operational paths. - Caveats: a user-built future-planning program or another organization&#39;s S4 could consume Porch telemetry, but is not a supplied first-party S4 constructor merely because telemetry exists.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity-level or ultimate-policy adjudication mechanism with legitimate decisive ownership and returned governance of Porch operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing permissible operating boundaries, provider identities, security exposure and project goals are limited by human-owned installation/configuration/task contracts, not resolved by a Porch ultimate-policy institution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: caller/user chooses task scope, external agent accounts, writable roots and provider configuration; per-run permission errors and acceptance decisions do not become identity-level policy arbitration. - Decision owner: no supported autonomous S5 owner or closed parent-governed S5 mode for the whole assessed system is evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: review read-only prompt, role/tool restrictions where provider supports them, external credentials/config, remote host security hardening, user authorization before parallel writer workspaces and ordinary acceptance criteria. - Closure path: provider permission refusal or task-scope concern → caller/user decision over that task; no shipped identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate top-level arbitration → authoritative update → subsequent Porch charter enforcement loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Porch has operational guardrails and user-set credentials/roots; a model applying those constraints does not author its own ultimate identity, and the parent can configure the tool without performing a defined S5 return loop. - Evidence: skill operating limits, delegate authorization/workspace contract, configuration, remote host security. - Basis: bounded first-party absence. - Confidence: high for absence of a defined identity authority path among inspected shipped runtime and policy surfaces. - Caveats: maintainers and users can revise the published skill, but contribution governance and configuration change are not a first-party operational S5 closure at this system recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ca22ec1154f38dc3128c0d2258d453ee0ab2644c/assessments/pr/pragmatic-orchestration.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ca22ec1154f38dc3128c0d2258d453ee0ab2644c\">ca22ec1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T16:55:51+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Aeven-AI/Aeven\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Aeven</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Aeven-AI/Aeven/tree/50e27b33594b7f0400fc666668fc1026c3fe7ca1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 50e27b33594b7f0400fc666668fc1026c3fe7ca1\">50e27b3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry user tasks through first-party model/tool and continuable child work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing user objectives, tool failures, incomplete artifacts, interruptions and missing context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next substantive tool/action, delegation or response from observed results. - Decision owner: The Aeven session model actor, with first-party spawned/forked child actors. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cordis plugin composition, queued turn/step driver, tool execution, durable session logs, filesystem/shell/browser/MCP and host permission gates. - Closure path: Session log → request assembly → model tool calls → execution results → next step; child messages/reporting return to the parent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The native loop owns iterative execution. Disabled Codex/Claude subagent rows and provider-internal control are not borrowed. - Evidence: packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml; packages/core/agent-loop/src/agent.ts; packages/core/agent-loop/src/tool-calls.ts; packages/subagent/tool-subagent/src/index.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed. - Boundary reachability: The released aeven preset mounts normal tools, native spawn/fork delegation and the first-party session loop; model/provider configuration completes ordinary deployment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Prevent writable interference between parallel work actors in the supported installed swarm/best-of-N mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent workers or competing attempts would otherwise share writable artifacts and contaminate each other before selection. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose nonoverlapping slices/separate output locations, keep attempts independent and decide subsequent retries/integration from returned evidence. - Decision owner: The parent model following the installed shipped skill; native subagent actors execute the separated briefs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: avn-swarm and avn-best-of-n packaged instructions, runtime subagents/workflow tools, filesystem/worktree commands and evidence-bearing reports. - Closure path: Parent allocates nonoverlapping briefs and private write locations → children act in their assigned locations → evidence/commit identity is checked → wrong/no-evidence results are rejected and respawned once or retained as gaps, then parent integrates accepted work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is explicit conflict attenuation with an agent-owned allocation/return policy, not S2 from generic messaging. No Agent Teams experimental service is needed. - Evidence: packages/skill/skill-catalog/authored/avn-swarm/SKILL.md; packages/skill/skill-catalog/authored/avn-best-of-n/SKILL.md; packages/skill/skill-catalog/README.md; packages/skill/skill-catalog/catalog/index.json; packages/host/account-integrations/src/index.ts; packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Optional account-installed catalog skills, not an always-active default guarantee; constraints are agent instructions, not filesystem sandbox proof.. - Boundary reachability: The official release skill catalog contains both ZIP bundles; Settings installCatalogSkill imports the bundled skill into account skills, which the supported session can load. Local bundle bytes were checked equal to authored instructions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate current descendant commitments and intervene on behalf of the root task organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Running/idle/ready descendants, corrupt or unavailable records, delayed work and tasks needing redirection or interruption. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose which current descendant work to interrupt and which direct child to steer or continue after inspecting current state. - Decision owner: The ancestor/root model actor within lineage-bound runtime authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listagents descendants projection, live/durable status, sendmessage, interruptagent, completion notification and authoritative lineage checks. - Closure path: Current tree/status/report → parent selects intervention/continuation → subagent service routes steering at a step boundary or requests cancellation → changed child activity and later reports return to the root. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The narrow credited function is current commitment regulation over the root task tree. It is not S3 from spawning alone, registry existence or a deterministic cancellation API without the model owner. - Evidence: packages/subagent/tool-subagent-control/src/list-agents.ts; packages/subagent/tool-subagent-control/src/index.ts; packages/subagent/subagent/src/control.ts; packages/subagent/subagent/src/continuation.ts; packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Interrupt is asynchronous, stops only the target current turn and does not stop its children or drain queued messages. No human parent modifier is inferred from browser steering.. - Boundary reachability: The default aeven preset mounts list/control alongside continuable spawn/fork children. These tools use the live calling agent and recorded lineage; no custom supervisor or experimental Team layer is needed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complement author claims with an independent raw-diff review and a returned correction/re-review loop in the installed review skill mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Self-reported completion can hide bugs, wrong callers or goal failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The fresh reviewer challenges the actual code/diff; the author accepts or rebuts findings with primary evidence, fixes accepted findings and returns the changed artifact for review. - Decision owner: Separate fresh reviewer model plus author model with explicitly separated review/edit rights; a human resolves only an unresolved terminal dispute. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: avn-review-loop packaged brief, fixed base/diff, raw code/tests, per-finding ledger, four-round bound and no-edit reviewer rule. - Closure path: Author result → fresh-context read-only reviewer inspects raw changed code → APPROVE/CHANGES with file:line claims → author fixes/rebuts and reruns checks → reviewer rechecks open IDs/changed code; failures/timeouts are not approval. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The independently framed critic has materially different access to the artifact rather than repeating author prose; both judgment and returned correction are packaged instructions using supplied model actors. Deterministic CI and routine Ralph reports are not the credited auditor. - Evidence: packages/skill/skill-catalog/authored/avn-review-loop/SKILL.md; packages/skill/skill-catalog/catalog/index.json; packages/skill/skill-catalog/README.md; packages/host/account-integrations/src/index.ts; packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Optional installed skill mode, not automatic S3 for every session. Actual run compliance or independent approval is not asserted by this source assessment.. - Boundary reachability: The release catalog ships avn-review-loop.zip, verified byte-identical to its authored SKILL.md. Supported Settings/account installation and skill loading make the workflow reachable without custom review code; it can use the default fresh spawn provider.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective harness adaptation function is supplied at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The environmental/authority variety potentially addressed by the inspected paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive right established in the assessed supported distribution. - Decision owner: No qualifying agent or parent owner established for this function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Background procedure distillation, user correction/slowness feedback, memory recall, capability candidates, lessons-to-checks and disabled self-extension tools. - Closure path: No corresponding function-specific organizational return loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The shipped learning path derives reusable procedures from completed tasks and corrections; it does not itself investigate prospective environmental alternatives or arbitrate them with present capability. Tool/web research remains S1 task execution unless a distinct adaptation case/return is established. Source-only developer self-extension and experimental packages do not donate an S4 mode. - Evidence: packages/capability/tool-capability/src/learner.ts; packages/capability/tool-capability/src/recall.ts; packages/capability/tool-capability/src/index.ts; packages/capability/tool-capability/README.md; packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml; packages/skill/skill-catalog/authored/avn-lessons/SKILL.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity or ultimate-policy arbitration loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The environmental/authority variety potentially addressed by the inspected paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive right established in the assessed supported distribution. - Decision owner: No qualifying agent or parent owner established for this function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persona/preferences, permission approvals, plan/goal requests, plugin/preset configuration and optional skill installation. - Closure path: No corresponding function-specific organizational return loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These define or configure the current assistant and authorize ordinary tasks/extensions; no runtime identity/ultimate-policy dispute is routed to a legitimate final authority and returned as a function-specific governing decision. Maintainer governance of Aeven releases is adjacent, not an operational S5 owner. - Evidence: packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/aeven/agent.cordis.yml; docs/architecture.md; packages/core/agent-loop/src/agent.ts; packages/experimental/README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/06fccd0f6b1a06204de177306ddf2f00e96abdf0/assessments/ae/aeven.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 06fccd0f6b1a06204de177306ddf2f00e96abdf0\">06fccd0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T21:37:50+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/steepy-it/steepy-apex\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Steepy Apex</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/steepy-it/steepy-apex/tree/7283b7940e6ec97ec739a28b6de1525d2891bdb4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7283b7940e6ec97ec739a28b6de1525d2891bdb4\">7283b79 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: directly implement and test bounded target-project changes, or a representative first application path, using code-aware model discretion under explicit task/goal context. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar repository APIs, failing tests, incomplete specs, integration edge cases, platform setup and differing source conventions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a model specialist selects the actual code/test edits, investigates source ambiguity, runs red→green→refactor, and may choose a changed fix from reviewer feedback; a Gear 4 model worker selects a mutation attempt inside controller-owned constraints. - Decision owner: host-backed task-specific &lt;surface&gt;-agent in Gear 3; headless Claude/Codex/OpenCode coding agent in supported autopilot/Gear 4; research/bootstrap model coordinator or bounded children during inception. Not the JavaScript event controller. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: routed standards, task briefs, host model-tier mapping, adapters/headless.mjs, diff snapshots, work manifests, deterministic verifier and path/commit guards, task reports. - Closure path: accepted plan task and real source → model specialist implements with TDD/tests → writes report/diff → scoped reviewer returns a correction or approval → changed source verified; Gear 4 repeats a model mutation only when the mechanical goal check/review/budget permits. - Boundary reachability: README.md installs these first-party skills into supported coding hosts; skills/implement/SKILL.md dispatches the prompt-bearing specialist; scripts/autopilot.mjs and scripts/loop-engineer.mjs invoke headless host models on their documented supported subset. The emitted code edits and repository tests occur in the actual user&#39;s project. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves explicit task headers, guards and test commands but no source-specific code design or repair decisions; deterministic keep/discard and audit receipt parsing are not credited as autonomous implementation. - Evidence: implementer model prompt, implement task dispatch/recovery, headless runner, Gear 4 implementation loop, inception bootstrap. - Basis: first-party role prompt plus installed host invocation and executable implementation/feedback routes. - Confidence: high for shipped model ownership contract; medium for cross-host live execution without provider credentials. - Caveats: simulated/fake-runner tests demonstrate state handling, not third-party provider correctness. On Pi/DeepSeek, Gear 3/4 headless runners are explicitly unavailable; host tool absence reduces a delegate to same-session model work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: detect and correct incompatibility across distinct S1 implementation tasks contributing to one branch. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task A changes an interface or owned shared surface and Task B depends on its old API, silently undoes the change, or violates a cross-cutting standard; separate task-local TDD may still pass. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a fresh model whole-branch reviewer evaluates aggregate task integration and reports specific ISSUESFOUND; the model implement coordinator chooses an owning task fix and re-dispatches a specialist, then orders a new cross-task review. - Decision owner: separate model reviewer for cross-task mismatch judgment and model implement coordinator for corrective work selection; local status parsers and serial dispatch only enforce/report. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: one-implementer-in-flight discipline, task-result index, success-criteria-only artifact, aggregate Git branch diff, cross-surface standards, final-review-issues.md and fix manifests. - Closure path: completed independent task results → aggregate diff/criteria/standards reviewed for mutually contradictory interfaces → actionable finding artifact → affected specialist model fixes the shared boundary → new aggregate diff, fresh independent review and final verification. - Boundary reachability: installed Gear 3 implement calls final-review-prompt.md, regenerates the branch diff and loops issues into a task-local fix; the documented autopilot phase uses validated task/final reviewer manifests and reviewer-result guards. This is not inferred from a generic task queue. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: explicit source-specific cross-task contract judgment and corrective task selection are model decisions, although task order and manifest/receipt constraints are deterministic. - Distinct S1 units: at least two separately bounded implementation tasks/possibly different &lt;surface&gt;-agent specialists in the same accepted plan, each owning an independent code/test deliverable (serial execution does not erase cumulative inter-task interference). - Inter-S1 disturbance: a later specialist&#39;s change to a shared interface or standards contradicts an earlier specialist&#39;s output, or its locally passing tests leave an aggregate integration fault. - Attenuating coordination relation: a model fresh-eyes whole-branch reviewer explicitly checks cross-task interface composition and contradictions against one aggregate diff, then supplies owning-task corrections. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: final-review-issues.md becomes a bounded fix manifest for the selected task&#39;s specialist; task output is revised, re-diffed and reviewed again. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the dedicated cross-task reviewer has the instruction to detect actual overlapping/interface incompatibility, and its issue artifact actively changes a task&#39;s implementation; mere one-at-a-time queueing or a status ledger does not supply the autonomous S2 witness. - Evidence: distinct task implementer role, whole-branch cross-task review, implement whole-branch fix closure, reviewer response evidence guards. - Basis: explicit cross-task interference/repair path, supported by source-level role separation and deterministic evidence validation. - Confidence: medium-high for live shipped model-mode relation. - Caveats: Gear 2 bypasses these fresh reviewers; same-session fallback on hosts without subagents weakens separation, and Gear 4&#39;s deterministic verifier alone should not be separately scored as S2.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: discretionary whole-current plan supervision, task/model-resource assignment, exceptional blocker escalation and whole-deliverable acceptance before handoff. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task complexity spread, unplanned discovery, one blocked specialist, incomplete planned work, review-fix churn, conflicting standards and over-spending strong model calls on mechanical changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the main model implement coordinator reads the accepted plan, chooses task-specific specialist/model tier within routing limits, responds to NEEDSCONTEXT/BLOCKED by targeted context, tier escalation or splitting, assigns final cross-task fixes and controls whether the whole planned branch is ready for the next review phase. - Decision owner: the model executing installed Gear 3 implement skill; human has ratified spec/plan and owns protected scope/publication. Gear 3 JavaScript autopilot does not steer models; Gear 4&#39;s deterministic attempt budget, verifier and commit/stop do not themselves furnish autonomous S3 discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: accepted plan/task dependency order, task/result ledger, per-surface model-tier mapping, reporter and reviewer output contracts, task-results.mjs, autopilot-context.mjs, reviewer-response.mjs, host role dispatch. - Closure path: all current plan tasks/reviewer statuses and resource needs → main model prioritizes local task/specialist and escalates review findings or blockers → bounded work/fix dispatch changes current commitments and subsequently updated ledger → whole-branch verified review and exact review-phase handoff. - Boundary reachability: ordinary installed Gear 3 implement has explicit supervisory instructions and runs in a model session; headless Gear 3 autopilot starts the same model phase as a real child with its routed manifest. This is not an attempt to credit the deterministic Gear 4 controller as a model supervisor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model decides actual current corrective assignments and risk-appropriate tiers under the whole plan, rather than a fixed plan→implement→review scheduler selecting those choices. Its human-approved scope remains binding; neither mere delegate invocation nor fixed budget logic is the evidence. - Whole-system current view: the coordinator sees the accepted multi-task plan, completed/pending ledger entries, task concerns, critical reviewer outcomes and whole-branch criteria/diff/approval status. - Current-control decision scope: sequencing and reassigning corrective work among specialists, model tier/escalation, splitting genuinely blocked tasks, and withholding next-phase completion until all task/branch gates are accepted. - Evidence: Gear 3 implement manager and escalation, model-tier/project flow, autopilot phase boundary, Gear 4 deterministic-rights separation. - Basis: explicit model-coordinator instructions with operational child/review return contracts, not the name controller. - Confidence: medium-high; no authenticated multi-model run executed during this assessment. - Caveats: this is Gear 3&#39;s model-owned current-control mode, not a claim that every host has full delegation; Gear 4 is deliberately a fixed deterministic loop after human goal/mutation authorization.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: an independent source/diff/test evidence path that can reject an implementer&#39;s apparent completion and force correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: optimistic implementer reports, hidden missing tests, divergence between task claims and actual changed code, aggregate integration regression and verifier gaming. - Decisive decision or feedback right: fresh read-only task and final reviewers use exact diff, applicable standards and criteria to decide APPROVED versus ISSUESFOUND; Gear 4&#39;s separately dispatched model branch reviewer may identify goal gaming that a deterministic verifier missed. - Decision owner: fresh model reviewer distinct from code-writing implementer/loop worker on supported task-tool/headless modes; deterministic receipt digests and test commands bind evidence, but do not decide the semantic verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: captured Git diffs, independent code read access, criteria-only context, reviewer reports/issue artifacts, reviewer-response strict schema/baseline checks, original terminal verifier output, mandatory validate-hub.mjs in later review. - Closure path: reported task completion → separate reviewer directly inspects code diff/source and checks plan requirements → approval or actionable issue file → main model controller re-dispatches fix and re-audits; final phase independently re-runs test/hub commands before PR offer. - Boundary reachability: skills/implement dispatches task-reviewer-prompt.md and final-review-prompt.md to fresh task tools where supported, and the headless Gear 4 controller dispatches a separate injected reviewer. These are installed, active role paths, not test-only fixtures. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer model owns substantive cross-check findings from a separate direct evidence path; the same-session fallback and deterministic validators are weaker and are not counted as the independent model case. - Claim being audited: one task/whole branch is correctly implemented, tested and compliant with approved criteria, or a Gear 4 apparent verifier success is genuine. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer&#39;s DONE/TDD report and work ledger plus a mechanical verifier&#39;s pass/fail. - Complementary access path: fresh reviewer reads task/aggregate Git diff and relevant repository source plus standards, with separate criteria-only manifest; Gear 4 supplies immutable branch snapshot and model review evidence not reducible to the verifier exit code. - Independence boundary: reviewer prompt is read-only and separate from code writer with a new context; it has independently derived diff/criteria inputs, not unlimited authority, and may use the same vendor/model family. If host has no task-tool, the documented inline pass is weaker and does not prove this mode. - Who acts on findings: implement model coordinator reads issue file, assigns task fixes and reruns review; deterministic Gear 4 controller can grant only budgeted follow-up mutation; later review skill withholds publication. - Evidence: task reviewer, whole-branch reviewer, Gear 4 independent reviewer, controller snapshot/feedback, final evidence gate. - Basis: direct-evidence separately prompted review with corrections fed back to operating work. - Confidence: high structural, medium live reviewer independence without authenticated host dispatch. - Caveats: a routine deterministic validate-hub test alone is not S3, and successful fake-runner/CLI tests are not evidence that every provider delivers a fresh independent model.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: in the first-party inception mode, research external technology/version support and compatibility options for a prospective target-project architecture, obtain legitimate parent selection, and return that choice as the current approved implementation baseline. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing runtime/framework/host support, unsupported/deprecated package versions, mismatched toolchains, future deploy constraints and incompatibility between a planned app and feasible external technology. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user/project owner confirms externally researched architecture alternatives and version combination; the model researcher gathers official-source evidence and options but does not own the foundational adaptation choice. - Decision owner: the legitimate human project parent in the dedicated inception research/architecture/approval mode. No agent-autonomous S4 alternative is established in this release. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: skills/inception/research-prompt.md official-source checks, version/date/support comparison table, decision register DR-n, helper-frozen approval/verification contract, component/coverage records, later certified hub import; model-mapping verifier detects host drift but does not automatically change shipped mappings. - Closure path: official ecosystem version/support/compatibility facts + future application/deploy requirements → model-supported option/research report → project human selects/approves foundation/version table → inception-state.mjs seals the approved project/contract → subsequent bootstrap uses selected versions and can be re-approved on substantial technology change. - Boundary reachability: inception is an installed skill invoked explicitly before init, with research step, user version-table confirmation, an approval gate and a real bootstrap/verification return. It is not Steepy maintainer roadmap or an example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: research may be model-run, but the consequential capability/stack adaptation choice is explicitly reserved for a parent; the model cannot silently pick unsupported versions or change approved foundation. - External distinction: current official releases, model/harness/framework support life cycles, compatibility constraints and upstream project/tool documentation are checked as external facts, not inferred from the local code tree. - Future / prospective distinction: chosen stack longevity, forthcoming deploy requirements and support/EOL risk are assessed as compatibility for the application&#39;s prospective operation, rather than only a current failing task. - Adaptation option generated: the researcher and model coordinator prepare version/framework alternatives with explicit support status, latest stable version, compatibility experiments and reasons for deviation; the parent selects a foundation. - Path back into current capability / S3: the parent-confirmed versions and architectural selection become a sealed project/verification contract, govern real bootstrap commands and current implementation, and require reapproval when a foundational choice must change. - Evidence: inception research/options and approval, model researcher from official external sources, whole lifecycle/parent version choice, state/approval enforcement, mapping drift explicit patch policy. - Basis: parent-governed prospective capability-selection/return loop, bounded to inception and major-stack reapproval. - Confidence: medium; this is targeted externally verified first-project capability formation, not an ongoing autonomous environmental intelligence agency. - Caveats: ordinary Gear 3 code discovery/brainstorm and routine model mapping drift alerts are not autonomous S4; follow-on hub work does not automatically re-run inception or adapt Steepy itself.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: ratify and preserve the target project&#39;s ultimate purpose, architectural boundaries, security/identity/access design and acceptable change policy, with parent arbitration and return into subsequent work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changed project scope or purpose, newly proposed auth/data/deployment boundary, conflict with existing hub standards, an unapproved fundamental technology/commit-policy change or a reduced verification requirement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the legitimate user approves complete project documents and contract, must re-approve substantial design/scope/identity/commit-policy shifts, and approves each promoted inception decision or later explicit doc-wins override; the model cannot self-approve. - Decision owner: project owner/operator as parent authority, not the worker, JavaScript status checker or autonomous implementation coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: frozen full-document approvals, stable DR-n decision register, inception-state.mjs strict guards, verification-contract checks, certified init governance, discovery per-item accepted write-back, persistent .apex/standards/conventions.md plus CONFLICT stop rule. - Closure path: identity-level architecture/purpose/permission change is surfaced in the inception decision register or as a hub conflict → legitimate parent selects/approves exact documents (or corrected hub preview) → helper freezes identity and gates bootstrap/change → approved decisions are certified/promoted to durable hub standards and subsequent model code work must obey them. - Boundary reachability: shipped inception user dialogue and inception-state approve are invoked on a real target project; init and discovery explicitly verify-and-promote the same approved decisions into the ordinary installed workflow&#39;s constraints. Parent S5 exists even when later Gear 4 coding is headless. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model is the proposer/coordinator and may recommend options, but ultimate project identity and altered governing rules require explicit human affirmation; this is an identity-level documentary/operational return, unlike a one-off PR permission prompt. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: system purpose and shape, identity/access and security policy, deploy/design boundaries, and changed foundational commitments require a different authorized project charter; this is not mere task approval. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: parent-governed inception is the only claimed positive S5 mode; the actual target-project human owner authoritatively approves complete architecture/policy documents and subsequent foundational revisions, and approves their hub promotion. - Return-to-operation path: helper-frozen project and verification contract constrain all bootstrap and future material changes; certified init and discovery promote approved decisions to .apex/ standards that govern later coverage/CONFLICT classification and code implementation. - Evidence: inception human approval/reapproval, inception approval commands and scope, certified policy return, verified governance promotion, doc-wins policy in normal workflow. - Basis: explicit, parent-governed identity-level choice plus recorded cross-mode return to future agent work. - Confidence: medium-high for shipped protocol; provenance of a human statement remains an operational trust assumption, as the helper explicitly notes. - Caveats: commit authorization, ordinary spec acceptance, PR offer, verifier pass/fail, and branch-only blast radius alone would not qualify as S5. The credited S5 path is deeper inception identity/reapproval → certified governed hub; it is not Steepy&#39;s OSS license/maintainer policy.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/44e7bcb8bf11ae8486406a23ba9998751555f51f/assessments/st/steepy-apex.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 44e7bcb8bf11ae8486406a23ba9998751555f51f\">44e7bcb &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T17:58:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/revfactory/codex-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harness for Codex</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/revfactory/codex-harness/tree/80f8217b5b6d3f220a31f05f9c0adaf09dac40f4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 80f8217b5b6d3f220a31f05f9c0adaf09dac40f4\">80f8217 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Explore source, design bounded implementation, write assigned code/tests or conduct narrowly scoped technical verification as actual project work units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown source architecture, technical errors, implementation alternatives, failing project tests, individual task acceptance details and changed inputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The project&#39;s model-backed explorer/architect/worker/QA agents choose source inspection, code modifications and test actions within a refreshed task packet and role permissions, returning findings, files and checks. - Decision owner: Native Codex subagent instantiated with a first-party harness role and assigned project-local packet; parent coordinates, and generic Codex model/tool runtime supplies inference/execution transport. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: .codex/agents TOML, plugin/skill invocation, work scopes, workspace-write or read-only role sandbox requests, native spawn/follow-up, run/input/result records, actual agent IDs and test commands. - Closure path: User goal → $harness target-project setup and model parent task breakdown → role-specific packet to native child → model-selected project read/write/check work → evidence/actual output to parent → accepted result or returned correction. - Boundary reachability: Plugin marketplace and skill explicitly enable target-project bootstrap; docs/multi-agent.md and skills/harness/SKILL.md describe actual native spawn and current-project prerequisites, including first-spawn preflight and operational fallback. Seed roles are valid TOMLs installed into the project, not merely benchmark sample agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The Python run ledger can check contracts and fingerprints but cannot select code edits or semantic analysis. The first-party agent role plus model execution is the operational owner; an unstarted agent definition alone would not be. - Evidence: installed plugin and $harness invocation; harness Phase 2/3 and first child preflight; bounded worker role; read-only explorer and QA roles. - Basis: Explicit installed model-backed native worker path and differentiated job rights; dynamic task actions returned to parent. - Confidence: High for shipped wiring, medium for live behavior across user-specific Codex clients and permission settings. - Caveats: Actual native role discovery, spawn capability, parent cwd and runtime limits must pass; source includes guarded fallback that must not be reported as a live multi-agent PASS. This assessment itself did not execute any model task.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Suppress actual/structurally evidenced collisions between two current task workers&#39; shared-file ownership, interface expectations or write succession, and return a revised conflict-free contract to affected S1s. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent worker A and B both trying to update a shared config/type/lockfile, incompatible interface assumptions, one worker&#39;s dependency output changing while another has begun, or competing edits to a shared workspace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The first-party parent is directed to assign one owner to shared files, serialize overlapping work, inspect a combined diff when concurrent contributions conflict, negotiate affected interface contracts and reassign/return updates to the appropriate workers. Peers report collisions and propose changes but do not silently overwrite. - Decision owner: Model-backed parent using explicit collaboration/ownership rules when mediating a concrete inter-worker conflict; deterministic dependency ledger and messaging helper support but do not decide semantic repair. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Disjoint ownership file sets, shared-interface agreements, actual native peer messages, parent-contract confirmation, ready/dependency checks, worktree preservation instruction, change fingerprint invalidation and recorded ownership transfers. - Closure path: Worker A&#39;s discovered shared-interface/file conflict with Worker B → direct peer event/parent report → parent examines source and shared contract, selects owner/serial order or revised interface → new current packets/explicit native follow-ups alter A/B actions → integrated source/test evidence closes the collision. - Boundary reachability: docs/multi-agent.md explicitly mandates single ownership for shared types/manifests/lockfiles and says out-of-scope edits go to the parent for reassignment/serialization. Its failure handling separately instructs the parent to inspect combined diffs after actual concurrent edits collide. harnessworker specifically reports a concurrent ownership overlap, and canonical skill Phase 5 makes parent contract confirmation/worker handoff part of supported runtime behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The S2 claim is not credit for a mailbox, task DAG or parent delegation. The parent makes a discretionary dispute-specific ownership/interface reconciliation based on different workers&#39; potentially incompatible changes; scripts merely record ownership and deliver refreshed packets. - Distinct S1 units: Two or more native harnessworker agents assigned separate project modules/parts of a dependency wave; independently execute and return their artifacts. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Both need the same shared interface/config/file, or one worker&#39;s new contract invalidates another&#39;s assumptions; concurrent edits would corrupt or contradict the integrated source. - Attenuating coordination relation: Single shared-file owner plus negotiated interface and serial handoff, with parent examination/reassignment if conflict is observed, not indiscriminate shared-write access. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Parent-confirmed revised ownership/dependencies and newly refreshed task packets dictate which worker may change the contested file and when dependent agents may proceed. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The intervention addresses a named inter-worker interference — overlapping writes/shared interface contradiction — and changes both workers&#39; subsequent allowed work; generic ready-queue execution or communication logging would not settle the contested source contract. - Evidence: shared types and out-of-ownership return; collision handling and parent reconciliation; worker explicit ownership-overlap return; skill Phase 2 and Phase 5 parent contract confirmation. - Basis: Differentiated first-party parent-owned inter-S1 collision mitigation with explicit returned file/contract changes. - Confidence: Medium; behavior is a real installed model-role contract, not independently measured actual conflict-resolution performance. - Caveats: run.py metadata validation alone does not choose a conflict resolution. File scopes constrain behavior by instructions, not OS-enforced per-path ACLs; agent mistakes remain possible and user policies may force manual repair.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Govern this project&#39;s current run across worker cells: priority/ready choices, commitment/ownership, available subagent capacity, integration, correction rounds and terminal acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Global task dependency drift, incomplete or stale outputs, multiple workers contending for current resources, model/tool incompatibilities, uneven progress, review/QA failures and inability to satisfy acceptance criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The parent Codex session reads the whole current goal, plan, dependencies, input fingerprints, accepted decisions, actual native agent states and returned evidence; it selects tasks to dispatch within capacity, verifies predecessor completion, returns specific defects to workers or performs permitted fallback, and accepts/blocks final integration. - Decision owner: First-party harness-configured parent model session, distinct from the deterministic run.py ready/result/resume checks and from an individual implementation worker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Run plan and task packets, actual native spawn IDs, 3-thread requested capacity with real runtime recheck, agents registry, dependencies and fingerprints, explicit message correlation, bounded retry limits and completion validator. - Closure path: Parent receives current state and worker/reviewer reports → chooses ready assignments, current interface/commitment correction, retry or escalation → native follow-up or agent launch directs S1 work → updated outputs/status, original tests and reviewer findings return → parent re-evaluates integration/closure. - Boundary reachability: Canonical skill Phase 5 makes the parent the active executor of task dispatch, current capacity control and final work acceptance after explicit invocation; repository .codex/config.toml and AGENTS pointer route the parent to the skill. Both plugin and installer modes have a documented path to actual Codex role/tool use. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Merely storing a plan, rate-limiting workers or printing a ready queue would not choose semantic task priorities, reconcile present interface changes or decide review-driven worker rework. Those choices are assigned to the executing model parent. - Whole-system current view: The one project run&#39;s objective, input fingerprints, dependency DAG, contracts, all registered live worker states, results, shared-file ownership, review and QA evidence, known blockers and remaining budget. - Current-control decision scope: Which task/role runs now, reallocation after failure/capacity, accepted interface changes, dependency readiness, updated work packets, bounded verification/rework and final current-run outcome; not product-market strategy or enterprise identity. - Evidence: parent role and actual scheduling/handoffs; Phase 5 integration and decision path; run registry and input freshness rules; configuration and target-project activation. - Basis: Active parent model with current-run-wide resource/commitment and rework decisions; deterministic state gives auditability, not autonomy on its own. - Confidence: Medium-high for the first-party mandate, moderate for live runtime compatibility in arbitrary Codex deployments. - Caveats: Parent must be in target-project cwd with actual role discovery/spawn tools; the source warns that absent native tooling leads to a limited fallback and no fabricated multi-agent success.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Challenge an implementer&#39;s source/correctness/completeness claims through a separate read-only reviewer and return causal findings into parent-controlled correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect or overstated worker success claims, missed regressions, mismatched interfaces, unsafe permissions assumptions, implementation artifacts that fail acceptance despite an apparently completed task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Separately instantiated harnessreviewer has a read-only context/role and directly examines assigned diff, surrounding code and acceptance criteria; it issues severity/file/line/trigger/impact/remedy findings to the parent. QA may independently run boundary tests and return actual check evidence. - Decision owner: The first-party reviewer role executed as its own native Codex subagent; parent uses returned findings to choose corrective assignment, while validate.py only checks structural/evidence completeness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: harnessreviewer.toml sandbox request read-only, direct diff/code inspection contract, refreshed review packet, no implementation rights, independent child context where supported, distinct QA role, returned findings and parent recheck loop. - Closure path: Worker edits project and reports success → parent commissions distinct read-only reviewer after integration → reviewer independently inspects real source/diff and acceptance boundaries → findings returned directly to parent → parent sends defects back to owning worker → changes and re-review/test outcomes update terminal acceptance. - Boundary reachability: Shipped reviewer TOML and canonical skill Phase 5/6 specify independent review after modules/integration. docs/multi-agent.md explicitly invokes reviewer/QA and routes defects to owning workers; plugin installation with actual native role use can instantiate the reviewer without developing a new capability. This is a normal bounded mode, not an external optional code-review vendor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewer makes new semantic judgments from original code evidence, rather than validating the implementer&#39;s own response or passing a static schema. The read-only role cannot silently rewrite its target change during review. - Claim being audited: Worker/parent&#39;s assertion that the changed code and current integration satisfy specified behavior and acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker result status, summary, changed files, own test report and current-run ledger. - Complementary access path: Distinct reviewer reads actual source/diff and criteria directly; QA can run observable tests independently, with findings not restricted to the worker&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: Dedicated harnessreviewer native role/context and requested read-only sandbox; shared Codex provider/models are possible, effective runtime policy may differ and the parent chooses reviewer task scope. - Who acts on findings: Parent owns acceptance and routes confirmed defects back to the implementation owner; worker implements corrections, then parent rechecks relevant boundary tests/audit. - Evidence: reviewer original diff and independent findings contract; QA role limits; integrate/review/route corrections; Phase 6 verification and rework. - Basis: First-party, separate reviewer with complementary raw artifact access and returned implementation correction loop. - Confidence: High for specified audit role/feedback, not measured reviewer effectiveness. - Caveats: A reviewer with read-only role may need a separately configured runtime to achieve effective isolation; no proof of model/vendor independence or live successful audit was generated by this Index assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Would autonomously scan the changing future environment, develop strategic capability options and return them to this task organization&#39;s current operating capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future model/tool ecosystem, user markets and external technology shifts beyond the declared project-local current work objective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Phase 0/1 inspects the target project&#39;s current source, Phase 2 chooses reusable local agent architecture, and Phase 7 handles user-requested drift/maintenance. No distinct future/outside intelligence owner with a standing prospective return loop is established at the current-run recursion. - Decision owner: Parent project operator instructs harness creation/updating and sets enduring purpose; first-party parent selects current local role/tool design to execute the requested task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Domain audit, role/skill creation, six architecture templates, explicit subsequent modification/drift review, retained project-local role instructions and validations. - Closure path: Current project inspection leads to reusable roles and skills used for the requested project tasks; later user-triggered audit can revise them. This is project-local capability provisioning, not an autonomous externally prospective strategy-to-current S3 return loop. - Boundary reachability: The explicit factory capability is real and operating, but targets a known current project and user request; generic potential use for future work does not show future external sensing and option selection. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-backed local harness design can be agent-owned S1 engineering work and may affect later executions, but that alone lacks the future environmental distinction, adaptation-option choice and S3 conversation required for S4. - Evidence: Phase 0/1 source audit and domain analysis; Phase 7 on-demand evolution and drift; actual bounded invocation contract. - Basis: Negative for external/future strategic intelligence; legitimate agent-authored present task&#39;s reusable infrastructure distinguished. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: At a differently defined long-term harness-maintenance organization, Phase 7 plus an explicit future environment sensing agent would merit a separate S4 review; no such owner was proven here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Would adjudicate the ultimate identity and legitimate governing purpose of the project organization, with binding return to current work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Deep disputes about mission, legitimate parent authority, principal values or constitutional constraints beyond ordinary code/task permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User request establishes the project/harness purpose, and inherited Codex tool/sandbox permissions determine allowable operations; the parent enforces current task contracts and can escalate, but does not originate ultimate policy. - Decision owner: Human project owner/operator and applicable higher-level Codex/user policy remain outside the first-party current work-run organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role sandbox requests, skill boundaries, user approval/consent, Codex parent session, project-local AGENTS.md, target-workdir safety, forbidden privilege escalation and tool availability checks. - Closure path: Parent follows existing user authorization and role permissions and may stop/escalate work; no evidence of an identity dispute deliberated and returned as an enduring policy decision across subsequent projects. - Boundary reachability: First-party permission and target-project checks are reachable and explicit, but they enforce already accepted instructions rather than empowering a separate legitimate-policy actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model role&#39;s current acceptance decisions and framework instruction texts are not authority to change organizational purpose; inherited permissions and human approvals are constraints, not a first-party S5 choice. - Evidence: user explicit invocation and project scope; parent responsibilities and platform permissions; AGENTS project guidance. - Basis: Negative for ultimate identity-policy authority at one project-local run recursion. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Parent model&#39;s final delivery answer and a user&#39;s approval are current task/parent inputs, not automatically published S5=P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f73dbb3d1779142b6c572cde0b6f330855e731b8/assessments/ha/harness-for-codex.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f73dbb3d1779142b6c572cde0b6f330855e731b8\">f73dbb3 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T16:39:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dragonked2/alphacode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AlphaCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dragonked2/alphacode/tree/a28db8978a5b336e244973c8db01f625dfcc0983\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a28db8978a5b336e244973c8db01f625dfcc0983\">a28db89 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform software-engineering and related browser/desktop automation through model-selected tools and actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar codebases, implementation choices, command/test failures, browser/desktop state, provider/model variation, task ambiguity and bounded worker assignments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select substantive coding/tool actions, interpret operational evidence and determine how to satisfy the assigned engineering objective. - Decision owner: the active model-backed AlphaCode coding agent; spawned swarm workers own their assigned operational workstreams. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in tool registry, provider routing, sessions, permissions/safety gates, context management, goal contracts, budgets and runtime cancellation. - Closure path: user/coordinator goal → model chooses coding/tool actions → first-party runtime executes → evidence returns → model revises implementation or reports completion. - Boundary reachability: ordinary AlphaCode sessions and spawned first-party swarm workers instantiate the same shipped coding runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code executes and constrains actions; the model owns the open-ended engineering decision. - Evidence: README; agent.rs; tool registry. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external MCP/browser/provider services remain dependencies and are not credited as S1 owners.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among concurrent swarm S1 units through model-selected graph decomposition, dependencies and assignment/takeover choices backed by first-party assignment and dependency enforcement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate active assignment, two workers concurrently editing overlapping work, dependency-order violations, overloaded workers, stale/dead assignees and integration/synthesis ordering. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task graph decomposition and dependency edges, select or request worker assignment, and explicitly reassign/replace/retry work when current peer interaction requires adjustment. - Decision owner: the model-backed swarm coordinator through the model-callable swarm tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan graph readiness, assignment-load ranking, in-flight target claims, active-assignment conflict rejection, worker heartbeats/staleness, dependency blocking/unblocking and typed node artifacts. - Closure path: coordinator defines graph/assignments → runtime observes peer assignment/dependency/liveness state → conflicting or non-runnable work is blocked/rejected/reclaimed → coordinator receives current graph/worker feedback and can reassign, retry, replace, spawn or restructure → affected S1 work proceeds under the revised relation. - Boundary reachability: the shipped swarm tool exposes taskgraph, assigntask, assignnext, runplan and task-control actions directly to the model; default swarm workers are first-party inline/headless AlphaCode sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime code enforces conflict and readiness rules, but the coordinator model chooses the organizational partition, dependencies and substantive takeover/recovery action. - Distinct S1 units: the coordinator/main coding agent and concurrently spawned first-party swarm worker coding agents, each owning a bounded task-graph node or workstream. - Inter-S1 disturbance: active double assignment can make two workers edit the same work concurrently; dependencies, worker load/liveness and overlapping responsibility can also cause unsafe or stranded work. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-selected task/dependency boundaries plus exclusive active assignment, load-aware worker selection, explicit takeover/reassignment and dependency gating. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: rejected double assignment prevents a second worker from starting; completion/failure/staleness changes graph readiness; reassign/replace tells the displaced worker to stand down and directs subsequent execution to another worker. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the path explicitly regulates concrete peer-worker interference and dependency/liveness conflicts rather than merely moving messages or decomposing a parent task. - Evidence: communicate.rs; commcontrol.rs; commgraph.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: low-level target claims and conflict rejection are deterministic support; the A classification rests on model ownership of graph/assignment/recovery choices.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current multi-worker organization by maintaining a whole-swarm view and intervening in active commitments, worker allocation, retries, replacement and recovery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failed, stale, crashed or overloaded workers; blocked graph nodes; incomplete artifacts; low-confidence work; current concurrency pressure; stranded assignments and changing ready frontiers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect whole-plan/member/context state and choose current assignment, spawn, stop, retry, reassign, replace, salvage, graph expansion or other current commitment action. - Decision owner: the model-backed swarm coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: planstatus/taskgraph summaries, member status, heartbeats, artifact metadata, readcontext/summary, runplan progress, task-control server transitions, concurrency budgets and resource guards. - Closure path: coordinator observes current plan plus worker status/artifacts → chooses current-control action → first-party server mutates assignment/worker/graph state and interrupts or dispatches workers as required → subsequent current execution reflects the decision. - Boundary reachability: the coordinator model receives the shipped swarm tool containing status, planstatus, summary/readcontext, runplan, spawn/stop and task-control actions; graph seeding can elect the seeding model session coordinator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: schedulers and resource guards enforce state transitions, but the model coordinator can inspect the whole current organization and choose the substantive commitment/recovery intervention. - Whole-system current view: swarm plan status exposes node readiness/status/dependencies/assignees plus low-confidence debt, while member status, heartbeats, summaries and readcontext expose current worker state and evidence. - Current-control decision scope: worker/task assignment and replacement, retries/salvage, spawning/stopping agents, concurrency use, graph expansion and current integration/synthesis commitments. - Evidence: communicate.rs; commcontrol.rs; commplan.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deterministic runplan loops and resource monitors also make current decisions, but the positive A claim does not depend on treating those enforcement components as organizational owners.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge completed deep-swarm work through separate critique/verify workers and force additional operational work when gaps remain. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete coverage, unsupported worker claims, low-confidence artifacts, missed edge cases and gaps hidden by ordinary worker completion reports. - Decisive decision or feedback right: probe the audited scope and either inject concrete follow-up nodes or issue a passing gate artifact accounting for every audited node. - Decision owner: the separate model-backed deep-mode critique/verify gate worker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: auto-inserted gate nodes, typed artifacts, audited-id accounting, low-confidence debt checks, completenode validation and injectgap graph mutation. - Closure path: production nodes complete with artifacts → independent gate worker receives an explicit audit scope and probes it → gaps cause injectgap and new operational nodes → after those drain the gate re-runs; only a sufficiently accounted clean artifact closes the gate → parent/composite work can then synthesize or finish. - Boundary reachability: deep taskgraph is a shipped swarm mode; deep expansion auto-inserts critique/verify gates that are dispatched through the same first-party worker infrastructure with gate-specific instructions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic server rules reject thin passes, but the separate gate model decides what to probe, whether a substantive gap exists and which follow-up nodes are needed. - Claim being audited: that the completed sibling/node artifacts sufficiently cover their assigned scope and are ready for synthesis/closure. - Ordinary reporting path: each production worker returns its typed completion artifact, validation, confidence and whatididnotcheck list. - Complementary access path: a separately dispatched full AlphaCode gate worker receives the aggregate audit scope, can use its own coding/repository tools to probe claims, and is instructed to seek gaps rather than accept the ordinary reports. - Independence boundary: the gate is a separate worker/model session with a distinct adversarial role and explicit audited scope; it is not the author worker checking its own completion. - Who acts on findings: the gate worker itself can inject new task-graph nodes; the coordinator/runtime dispatches those nodes and prevents the parent scope from closing until they drain. - Evidence: swarm core; commcontrol.rs; commgraph.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the ordinary deterministic report claim checker is not used for this state; S3 rests on the separate adversarial deep-gate worker with direct first-party operational tooling.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established at the assessed swarm/coding recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying future/external distinction is shown being turned by a distinct adaptation owner into options that return to change current organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent memory, web/browser tools, ambient scheduling, selfimprove/selfdev tools, provider/tool discovery and project-analysis helpers may influence later work but are not sufficient S4 closure. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: self-improvement and memory can change or preserve behavior, but the Profile explicitly requires an external-and-prospective option-development conversation with present capability; the inspected paths remain task/current-repository, persistence or scheduled-execution mechanisms. - Evidence: memory tool; selfimprove tool; ambient manager. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: web/ambient/selfdev surfaces are powerful extension and automation mechanisms, but no standard future-oriented adaptation loop into S3 was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established at the coding/swarm recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level or ultimate-policy conflict is shown reaching an authoritative owner and returning as governing runtime policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: safety gates, destructive-action checks, permissions, system prompts, configuration, goals and resource budgets constrain ordinary operation but do not create identity authority. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents can adapt tactics and organize workers but cannot authoritatively redefine AlphaCode&#39;s identity or ultimate operating principles. - Evidence: README safety section; tool registry; resource monitor. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user configuration/approval and safety policy are legitimate constraints but do not establish S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6dac75ca3fbfaf4412ec9666b6681415c7477b9a/assessments/alphacode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6dac75ca3fbfaf4412ec9666b6681415c7477b9a\">6dac75c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-05T22:29:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hizliemre/horse-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Horse Code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-05\">2026-10-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hizliemre/horse-code/tree/8e134ac3b2745477940bda5867313d07ae535900\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8e134ac3b2745477940bda5867313d07ae535900\">8e134ac &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously implement software-development tasks through model-selected repository/tool actions until reviewed work is ready to merge. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar codebases, implementation choices, repository constraints, build/test failures, review feedback, tool/model failures and merge-local changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose concrete code changes and tool actions, interpret returned repository/test evidence, revise the implementation and decide when a task attempt is ready for review. - Decision owner: the active Horse Code implementer role agent (for example coder, designer, senior-coder or principal-coder depending on the stage). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role registries, tool permissions, isolated worktrees, board state, model fallbacks, turn budgets, automatic checkpoints, code-review/acceptance gates and git merge machinery. - Closure path: task + project context → implementer chooses tool/code actions → worktree produces concrete results → review/test feedback returns → implementer/revision actor changes work → accepted work merges into the session base. - Boundary reachability: standard feature/bugfix jobs construct role agents and task worktrees through the shipped job, wave-task, task-cycle and agent-loop paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic worktree, board and git machinery transports and constrains the attempt, but removing the role model removes the discretionary coding choices that create the implementation. - Evidence: README.md; src/engine/task-cycle.ts; src/engine/wave-task.ts; src/agent/loop.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: role/model routing and runtime guards can change which agent serves an attempt, but they do not replace the implementer&#39;s open-ended operational decision right.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among parallel implementer S1 units before and during concurrent work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two operational task agents may be scheduled together despite an unstated semantic dependency or may attempt to write overlapping files, causing stale assumptions, failed downstream tasks or merge conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge whether candidate parallel tasks have a missing dependency that makes simultaneous execution unsafe and add the dependency edge that changes their subsequent scheduling. - Decision owner: the model-backed team-lead role for semantic dependency coordination. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project-manager file/dependency declarations, deterministic computeWaves, splitFileConflicts, live busy-file exclusion, dependency-ready scheduling, isolated worktrees and serialized merge sections. - Closure path: board exposes candidate parallel tasks/dependencies/write paths → team-lead audits the interaction → accepted missing dependency is added to the board → wave computation/scheduler observes the new edge → affected implementer S1s no longer run in the conflicting relation. - Boundary reachability: team-lead audit is invoked by the standard board/wave pipeline before parallel implementation and writes directly to the first-party Board consumed by execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the scheduler and file exclusion deterministically enforce already-known relations; the semantic decision that a task actually needs another task&#39;s output is produced by the team-lead agent. - Evidence: src/engine/project-manager.ts; src/engine/team-lead.ts; src/engine/waves.ts; src/engine/wave-engine.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: same-file exclusion is deterministic support and is not used by itself to claim agent ownership. - Distinct S1 units: parallel coder/designer/other implementer agents executing different board cards in separate task worktrees. - Inter-S1 disturbance: missing semantic dependencies and overlapping write sets can make concurrently executing cards interfere, consume stale state or collide at merge. - Attenuating coordination relation: team-lead adds missing task dependencies; deterministic wave/file-clash machinery then serializes the affected relationship. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the changed Board dependencies and busy-file relation alter eligibility, so an affected implementer starts later against merged predecessor state instead of continuing in the unsafe parallel relation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited path is tied to a concrete interaction disturbance between distinct parallel operational units and changes their interaction topology; generic board storage and role routing are not the positive witness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: regulate current whole-job resource pressure by changing how many operational task agents may run concurrently. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a job can expose more runnable cards than the available machine/model/provider capacity can safely support, producing heap pressure, rate limits and a larger unfinished tail. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the live maximum number of task agents allowed in flight for the current/future scheduling passes of the running job. - Decision owner: the human operator in the supported interactive TUI mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TUI board/running-agent display, /parallel N, the mutable parallelRef, persisted maxParallel, wave-engine slot accounting and the scheduler&#39;s live ceiling() getter. - Closure path: current run exposes active agents/board state → operator chooses a new concurrency ceiling → setParallel updates the live value → the scheduler rereads that ceiling on subsequent passes → current task admission/resource usage changes. - Boundary reachability: /parallel is a shipped TUI command explicitly documented to take effect on the running job. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic scheduling enforces the ceiling, but the discretionary whole-run resource choice belongs to the operator. No first-party autonomous actor was established that owns an equivalent whole-system current resource/commitment judgment. - Evidence: README.md; src/tui/commands.ts; src/tui/app.tsx; src/engine/wave-engine.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: team-lead dependency repair is credited to S2, while deterministic park/wake/retry, model-health and slot machinery support present operations without owning S3 discretion. - Whole-system current view: the TUI surfaces the persistent board and currently running agents/models for the job while the operator can issue run-control commands. - Current-control decision scope: the operator changes the global in-flight task ceiling that governs resource allocation across the job, with immediate effect on later scheduling passes.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the integrated result after local task review and return whole-diff findings into corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: locally reviewed task outputs can each look acceptable while their merged combination still contains cross-task defects, deferred review concerns or integrated-surface problems. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the merged base diff holistically and decide approve versus request-changes; after bounded revision rounds, decide whether remaining findings are acceptable or require a human decision. - Decision owner: the separate model-backed principal-coder reviewer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only reviewer tools, merged/base diff construction, persisted revision card, PR review comments/threads, senior-coder revision loop, commit/push and repeat review. - Closure path: task-level work merges → principal-coder receives the integrated diff plus deferred findings → autonomous audit verdict/comments → senior-coder applies changes → changes are committed/refreshed → principal re-reviews or makes a bounded final audit decision. - Boundary reachability: the standard job path invokes the PR revision pass whenever merged work exists, even on partial jobs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the principal-coder is a distinct read-only judgment role from the operational implementers and from the senior-coder that applies corrections; git/thread machinery only records and transports its findings. - Evidence: src/engine/job.ts; src/engine/revision.ts; src/engine/reviewer.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary per-task code review and acceptance gates are not independently sufficient for S3. The positive witness is the additional integrated post-merge audit over the combined result. - Claim being audited: that the merged job result, not merely each individual task, is coherent and ready to ship. - Ordinary reporting path: task implementers plus per-task code review/acceptance report individual card outcomes and merge them into the base. - Complementary access path: the principal-coder later reads the combined base/PR diff holistically, including deferred local findings. - Independence boundary: principal-coder is a separate read-only reviewer from the task implementers and the senior-coder revision actor, and it observes the integrated artifact after local review/merge. - Who acts on findings: senior-coder applies requested corrections; the principal-coder then re-audits the changed integrated result, with unresolved ownership decisions eventually reaching the user.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: adapt Horse Code&#39;s future role capability to changing external model availability/capability and project-specific skill needs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: connected model sources change availability, quota, cost/capability and source diversity; projects differ in whether skills such as testing or UI guidance are appropriate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose model chains for all roles from the currently healthy external model pool and choose which installed skills should become durable role capabilities for the scanned project. - Decision owner: the model-backed tuner used by /roles adjust (with a separate model-backed skill tuner for skill assignment). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model-source discovery, model-health quarantine/probing, capability/source heuristics, project factual scan, assignment validation, role registries and config persistence. - Closure path: current external model pool + project facts → tuner reasons over role requirements and adaptation choices → validated model/skill assignments are applied to live role registries → saved configuration changes future sessions and later S1/S2/S3 operation. - Boundary reachability: /roles adjust is a shipped TUI path; it calls the first-party tuners, applies their returned chains/skills and persists them. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic validators constrain invalid or unsafe assignments, but the role-to-model and role-to-skill selection is produced by model reasoning rather than fixed configuration or the operator choosing each assignment. - Evidence: src/tui/app.tsx; src/engine/role-tuner.ts; src/engine/skill-tuner.ts; src/engine/project-scan.ts; src/engine/model-health.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the operator explicitly invokes /roles adjust; that trigger is not the decisive adaptation judgment. Manual /roles setmodel and config edits are not separately promoted to a parent S4 mode here. - External distinction: which connected/provider-backed models are currently healthy/routable and what installed capability/skill inventory is available. - Future / prospective distinction: which role-capability allocation will better survive later workload, rate-limit/source failures and project-specific task demands. - Adaptation option generated: a validated three-model chain per role plus project-appropriate skill assignments. - Path back into current capability / S3: assignments are applied to live role registries and persisted, altering the agents/resources available to later operational, coordination, review and control activity.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: establish or amend the project&#39;s standing constitution so later Horse Code roles are governed by the returned project-level principles. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a project may need new or revised standing principles, coding/governance rules or identity-level constraints that ordinary task execution should not decide locally. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine the requested project-governance/constitutional direction and any core-principle choices that the analyst cannot legitimately infer. - Decision owner: the human user/operator as legitimate parent project authority in the supported govern path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: intent classification to govern, analyst constitution phase, askuser, committed constitution artifact, constitution parsing/scoping/caching and rule injection into role prompts. - Closure path: user raises a governance/constitution matter → analyst carries that request and asks the parent when core principle decisions are needed → constitution is established/minimally amended → subsequent role prompts receive the applicable rules → later operation is governed by the returned project policy. - Boundary reachability: the govern intent and constitution phase are shipped first-party lanes, and ordinary feature runs also load the same constitution into governed role prompts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the analyst authors and operationalizes the document, but the legitimate ultimate authority for project principles remains the user; automatic initial constitution authoring is not treated as evidence that the analyst has acquired ultimate project identity authority. - Evidence: src/engine/upstream.ts; src/speckit/phases.ts; src/engine/constitution.ts; src/engine/constitution-store.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a static constitution file alone would not establish S5. The positive path is the supported parent governance lane plus return of the authoritative result into later runtime prompts. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: establishment or amendment of the project&#39;s own standing principles and governing rules rather than an ordinary implementation choice. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: the human project user/operator is the ultimate authority in the claimed parent-governed mode; no separate autonomous S5 base mode is claimed. - Return-to-operation path: the resulting constitution is stored in the project, scoped into applicable rules and injected into later agent prompts so subsequent work is governed by that decision.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f0d20ee58a50fe8c6c03f2bcaf8fa1091c7d8fd0/assessments/horse-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f0d20ee58a50fe8c6c03f2bcaf8fa1091c7d8fd0\">f0d20ee &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-30T13:28:42Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ApodexAI/FrontierAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FrontierAgent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-30\">2026-09-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ApodexAI/FrontierAgent/tree/9e533db6f6c34d16037ee5ec964c479d0eb51cde\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9e533db6f6c34d16037ee5ec964c479d0eb51cde\">9e533db &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: independently perform bounded open-ended research, analysis, coding/file and evidence-gathering assignments using model reasoning and tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing task instructions, web/file/tool observations, failures, intermediate evidence, sandbox state, assigned output constraints and follow-up questions from the coordinator. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next task-specific search, tool, file, computation or report action and revise later actions from returned observations within the delegated assignment. - Decision owner: each model-backed Agent Team sub-agent session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SwarmSubagentRuntime; first-party agent loop; per-session specialist prompts; allowed-tool sets; private worktrees; task/session budgets; submitreport; context compaction/recovery; AgentBus dispatch. - Closure path: coordinator assignment + original question → sub-agent model chooses task-specific actions → first-party tool/runtime executes them → observations return to that sub-agent session → the model revises its approach until it submits a report or reaches a runtime stop condition. - Boundary reachability: Agent Team is a documented native workflow in README.md and ships ready profiles whose coordinator can create/assign sub-agents with first-party research/file tools; no user-authored orchestration code is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the sub-agent model while retaining AgentBus, sandboxing, queues and tool executors removes the open-ended choice of what evidence/action to pursue next. Deterministic runtime components constrain the work but do not own its task-specific decisions. - Evidence: README.md; subagentruntime.py; assigntask.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deterministic recovery/finalization fallbacks can terminate a failed sub-agent, but ordinary task ownership remains model-backed and feedback-driven.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate concrete interference among distinct S1 work units, including contradictory evidence and competing authority to publish the same final deliverable. - Distinct S1 units: independently running model-backed Agent Team sub-agent sessions assigned separate research/file tasks, including parallel investigators, a selected publisher, and specialized verifier sessions. - Inter-S1 disturbance: distinct sub-agents can return contradictory claims/derivations, and independently active workers could otherwise contend for the same final /outputs deliverable or retain stale publication authority after reassignment. - Attenuating coordination relation: the coordinator routes disagreements to a dedicated localverifier with both reports attached and assigns follow-up work from that arbitration; for output contention it grants exactly one structured outputpaths manifest while runtime publication state/locking excludes competing publishers and forces all others workspace-only. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: verifier findings change which claim the coordinator accepts and what follow-up assignments it dispatches; publication arbitration changes which sub-agent can write /outputs, which agents remain workspace-only, and whether publisher work is reused, delayed, or transferred. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited paths regulate identified interference among otherwise autonomous S1 outcomes/commitments and close the resolution back into those units&#39; later work or write authority; mere fan-out, messaging and the task board are not used by themselves as S2 evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incompatible sub-agent reports/derivations; overlapping or contradictory evidence; concurrent attempts to own/write the run&#39;s final output manifest; stale publisher ownership during reassignment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the coordinator decides when a disagreement requires separate arbitration and which S1 receives follow-up work/publication authority; a dedicated localverifier independently resolves the contested claim, while runtime publication guards enforce the coordinator&#39;s single-publisher allocation. - Decision owner: the model-backed Agent Team coordinator for coordination strategy and assignment; the separately model-backed localverifier for the disputed-evidence arbitration it is assigned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: teameffort: max conflict policy; &lt;attach agent=&quot;...&quot;/&gt; report transfer; LOCALVERIFIER specialist prompt; task-board owner changes; structured outputpaths grant; publication lock/state; one-publisher guard; workspace-only routing for non-publishers; verifier publish prohibition. - Closure path: distinct S1 reports conflict → coordinator detects the disagreement and spawns/assigns localverifier with both reports → verifier re-derives/source-checks and returns an arbitration report → coordinator updates the task/evidence plan and later assignments from that result. For file contention, coordinator grants one exact output manifest → runtime blocks overlapping publisher ownership → affected agents execute either publisher or workspace-only roles accordingly. - Boundary reachability: all three shipped Agent Team profiles set teameffort: max, which injects the conflict-verifier strategy into the coordinator contract; assigntask and its publication guards are first-party tools in those shipped profiles. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic locks alone would only serialize access, but here the model-backed coordinator decides which evidence conflict to arbitrate, whether to dispatch more work and which agent owns the publisher commitment. Removing that agent-owned decision while retaining the lock/task board removes the adaptive coordination policy. - Evidence: prompts.py; assigntask.py; taskboard.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: not every parallel assignment is S2. The positive finding is limited to the concrete conflict/arbitration and publication-interference loops above.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a current whole-run view of work and regulate present commitments, staffing and priorities across the Agent Team. - Whole-system current view: the coordinator receives the run-wide task board with open/in-progress/resolved/cancelled commitments and owners, live AgentBus session/job status, collected/failed/running reports, publication ownership, and remaining runtime/budget signals across the Agent Team. - Current-control decision scope: the coordinator can add/cancel/reopen tasks, replace owners, create/reuse/stop sub-agents, dispatch another evidence wave, wait or proceed, and select/transfer the final publisher, thereby changing current resource allocation and operational commitments across the run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfinished or newly emergent tasks, weak evidence, stalled/off-track agents, exhausted sessions, failed assignments, conflicting reports, publication responsibility and shrinking runtime/time budgets. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which work remains open/resolved/cancelled, add new work, choose/replace owners, dispatch or reuse agents, stop an active agent, launch another evidence wave, wait for reports, and select/transfer the final publisher subject to first-party safety guards. - Decision owner: the model-backed Agent Team coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live task board with resolutions/owners and agent/job status; createsubagent; assigntask; collectreports; stopsubagent; addtask/updatetask; AgentBus; SpawnGuard; session/task caps; wall/budget observers; publication-state guard. - Closure path: task board + live agent/job/report state → coordinator compares current coverage/commitments with the user objective → coordinator changes owners/status, dispatches follow-up/new agents, stops/reuses/reassigns work or changes publisher → AgentBus/task-board/publication state applies those choices → subsequent S1 work proceeds under the revised commitments and returns new reports/state. - Boundary reachability: the documented native Agent Team workflow exposes these tools to the coordinator in shipped simple, tui and benchmark profiles; the task board is always on even though shipped profiles disable the optional two-phase planning mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: AgentBus, task limits and the board mechanically represent/enforce state, but the coordinator chooses which current disturbance matters and how to reallocate commitments. Removing the coordinator model leaves resource caps and queues but removes adaptive whole-run prioritization/reassignment. - Evidence: README.md; workflows/agentteam/README.md; taskboard.py; stopsubagent.py; assigntask.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the S3 finding is not based on delegation or result aggregation alone; it is based on the coordinator&#39;s current whole-run state plus actual commitment/reassignment/stop/publication rights.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the integrated answer/evidence before completion and return corrective findings to current control. - Claim being audited: the coordinator&#39;s integrated draft and its load-bearing factual, computational and sourced claims before the Agent Team presents the final answer/deliverable. - Ordinary reporting path: research/worker sub-agents return assignment reports to the coordinator, which synthesizes those reports into the current draft and manages the ordinary task board/commitment loop. - Complementary access path: a separately instantiated finalverifier receives the original question plus the complete draft, then uses its own verifier prompt and independent/different queries, source checks or re-derivations rather than merely accepting the workers&#39; reports. - Independence boundary: verifier role names are routed to a dedicated first-party verifier system prompt, verifiers are prohibited from publishing the deliverable, and the audit runs in a separate model-backed session from the coordinator and ordinary workers; the optional planning gate can additionally require verifier participation. - Who acts on findings: the model-backed coordinator consumes verifier findings, repairs the synthesis or dispatches new S1 research on failed points, updates present commitments, and can send the corrected result through another verification pass. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported or incorrect final claims, arithmetic/factual errors, weak sourcing, contradictions surviving synthesis and other defects in the coordinator&#39;s draft. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the separately instantiated finalverifier decides whether claims survive independent re-derivation/source checking and reports concrete failures; the coordinator must then repair/research the failed part and can re-verify before finalizing. - Decision owner: the model-backed finalverifier sub-agent for audit findings; the coordinator owns the corrective response to those findings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reserved verifier role names; automatic specialist prompt routing; FINALVERIFIER role contract; independent/different-query verification guidance; verifier publication prohibition; report handoff; coordinator repair/re-verification loop; optional planning-mode finalizegate that additionally hard-blocks completion without verifier participation. - Closure path: coordinator forms a complete draft → creates/assigns finalverifier with the original question and draft → verifier independently checks load-bearing claims from sources/re-derivation and returns a report → coordinator repairs substantive gaps by revising synthesis or dispatching another S1 wave → corrected answer can be re-verified before completion. - Boundary reachability: all shipped Agent Team profiles set teameffort: max, whose standard coordinator contract explicitly calls for a final verifier and repair/re-verification. Optional planning mode strengthens this with a deterministic finalize gate, but that non-default gate is supporting evidence rather than the sole basis of the positive state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verifier is a separate model-backed session with a dedicated verifier prompt and no publication authority; it does not merely echo the coordinator&#39;s normal synthesis path. Removing the verifier model leaves logging/guards but removes the independent challenge and finding generation. - Evidence: prompts.py; createsubagent.py; assigntask.py; finalizeanswer.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: shipped profiles keep planningmode: false, so the verifier requirement is model-contract enforced there rather than deterministically blocked by finalizegate. The verifier itself is nevertheless a first-party, separately prompted, boundary-reachable audit actor with corrective return.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented adaptation loop established at the declared Agent Team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate adaptation signals include changing external information, repeated task outcomes, benchmark results, runtime failures, reusable session history, skills and operator steering. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor was found that owns external/future modelling and converts it into a persistent change to FrontierAgent&#39;s operational strategy, tool/skill repertoire or organizational configuration, then returns that adaptation through S3. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web research; reusable sub-agent sessions; context/history/checkpoints; skills loading; benchmark/evaluation surfaces; user steering; profile configuration. - Closure path: not established. Agents can research external facts for the current task and retain/reuse current-run context, while skills/profiles are loaded from configured artifacts; no standard first-party loop was found that autonomously generates/selects and persists a future-oriented organizational adaptation for later operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-task research and evidence-driven follow-up are S1/S3 activity, not S4 by themselves. Memory/checkpoints and operator-supplied skills preserve or configure capability but do not create an agent-owned outside-and-then adaptation conversation. - Evidence: README.md; workflows/agentteam/README.md; frontieragent/components/skills/; subagentruntime.py. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the runtime has useful ingredients for future adaptive machinery, but ingredients and generic external access are not credited as S4 without the complete prospective adaptation closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy closure established for one Agent Team run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate policy matters include tool permissions, approval requirements, execution/sandbox policy, role naming, model/profile configuration and publication authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party agent or parent path was found with authority to resolve identity/purpose/ultimate-policy questions for the runtime and return the result as authoritative policy governing subsequent operation. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: operator-authored profiles; execution/tool permissions; approval gates; sandbox constraints; role/session identities; publication manifests; runtime limits. - Closure path: operational policy/configuration constrains tools and writes, but it is supplied/enforced as configuration. No identity-level issue is escalated to legitimate ultimate authority and returned as a policy decision by the assessed runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: technical agent/session identity, system prompts, permissions and approval enforcement are operational boundaries. They do not by themselves constitute S5 identity and ultimate-policy ownership. - Evidence: README.md; workflows/agentteam/profiles/; assigntask.py. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is a runtime-recursion finding; it does not claim the OSS project or its maintainers lack human governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f7ed4df5f68a71f3d2fd4c93f371058c05ef8593/assessments/frontieragent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f7ed4df5f68a71f3d2fd4c93f371058c05ef8593\">f7ed4df &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T20:10:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Loqira-Labs/agentkot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">KOT</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Loqira-Labs/agentkot/tree/7cf18c4b8389a81ea2f15ab70d1b0bb31b44b6ab\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7cf18c4b8389a81ea2f15ab70d1b0bb31b44b6ab\">7cf18c4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform user objectives into code/file/process/research outcomes through repeated model-selected tool actions and returned operational evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user requests, changing workspace/process state, tool errors/results, provider/model outputs, prior session evidence, context pressure and follow-up input. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive tool/action, arguments and stopping point, revising later actions from returned tool/environment evidence. - Decision owner: the active KOT session model; child models own the same local operational discretion within their assigned work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shipped executable, built-in tool schemas/executors, persistent sessions, History/Memory/Plan, background-process management, provider adapters and Web UI. - Closure path: user/session context → model selects tool/action → KOT executes and records the result → later model step observes that evidence → model revises the next operation or concludes. - Boundary reachability: the executable and built-in model/tool surface are the standard shipped product, not benchmark/development-only machinery. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model removes task-specific selection of tools, arguments and completion; the runtime executes and persists those choices rather than replacing them with a fixed procedure. - Evidence: README.md; tools/Agent.md; built-in tool documentation. - Basis: explicit -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among concurrently executing child S1s by assigning dependencies/owners and, when operations may collide in a shared project, selecting isolated worktree execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ready workflow steps/delegates/teammates can operate concurrently; without isolation, inherited child cwd and disk-synced edits can overlap in the same project state and invalidate peer work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which child work may run concurrently and whether a child/step receives isolation:&quot;worktree&quot; / transactional worktree execution, plus define task dependencies that delay dependent work. - Decision owner: the orchestrating lead model when issuing Agent/Plan calls; for declared workflows, the first-party workflow declaration supplies the chosen dependency/isolation relation and KOT enforces it. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate child sessions, git worktree creation/cleanup, workflow DAG dependency scheduler, shared Plan board, serialized task mutation and child result/mail feedback. - Closure path: lead identifies/decomposes multiple tasks → assigns dependencies and/or selects a dedicated worktree for an interfering child → KOT schedules ready work and enforces isolated cwd/branch where selected → child results/status return to the lead/shared board → subsequent work is integrated, redirected or delayed accordingly. - Boundary reachability: Agent{spawn|delegate|hire} and workflow transactional/worktree controls are documented built-in capabilities of the shipped executable; no downstream coordinator is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: KOT deterministically creates the requested worktree and respects declared DAG edges, but the task-specific judgment that work is independent or needs isolation/dependency belongs to the lead model/declaration. The runtime does not infer equivalent semantic isolation from the task text. - Evidence: tools/Agent.md; tools/Plan.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: worktree isolation is optional; credit is for the shipped model-selectable coordination relation, not for generic concurrency or messaging alone. - Distinct S1 units: the lead session plus multiple separately running child/delegate/teammate sessions, each driven by its own model and tool loop. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrently ready children may otherwise edit the same inherited project workspace or consume/alter shared operational state in incompatible ways. - Attenuating coordination relation: model/declaration-selected git-worktree isolation, plus explicit workflow/Plan dependencies and owners that constrain which work becomes ready together. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child terminal notifications, mail, shared task-board state and workflow state return coordinated outcomes; failed/dependent/isolation outcomes alter later lead/child work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation targets a concrete concurrent shared-workspace interference mode and changes where/when affected S1 units may operate; mail and child spawning alone are not credited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current portfolio of owned child/delegate/teammate work, including commitments, lifecycle, task ownership and model/provider resources. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing child progress/liveness, pending mail, failed or obsolete commitments, blocked tasks, provider/model suitability, workflow pause/failure and need to redirect or terminate current work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create/withhold child commitments; inspect current owned-agent status; send new instructions; stop work; change a live teammate&#39;s model/provider; mutate task owners/dependencies; inspect/stop/resume workflows. - Decision owner: the lead/orchestrating model for its owned domain, with optional human ownership only where an explicit workflow ask/escalation step is declared. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent status/send/stop/setmodel, durable child registry/session ids, shared Plan board, workflow status/stop/resume, mail router and Web current-state panels. - Closure path: lead creates current commitments → status/task/workflow views return present state → lead chooses steering, reassignment, resource/model change, stop or further delegation → KOT applies it → subsequent child/workflow operation changes. - Boundary reachability: these operations are model-facing built-ins of the shipped Agent/Plan surface and are also exposed in standard Web controls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: lifecycle services enforce stop/mail/model transitions, but the substantive current-control choice over which commitment to redirect, terminate or re-resource is made by the lead model. - Evidence: tools/Agent.md; tools/Plan.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: high - Caveats: KOT intentionally prevents arbitrary child re-orchestration; S3 is centered at the main lead session&#39;s declared ownership domain. - Whole-system current view: Agent{status} lists the lead&#39;s teammates and delegates with lifecycle/live state, model/provider, pending mail and wake errors; the Plan board and workflow status expose current task ownership/dependencies/run state for the same organization. - Current-control decision scope: child/team creation, current instruction steering, interruption/removal, task ownership/dependency mutation, workflow stop/resume and live teammate model/provider allocation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a delegated worker&#39;s claim/readiness for completion against direct operational checks and, when configured, a separate judge verdict before the loop is accepted. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a worker can report plausible completion while the actual workspace fails the declared command/test or while a separate judge identifies a material reason to continue/escalate despite a passing direct check. - Decisive decision or feedback right: after direct working-directory verification, independently choose accept, continue with corrective specifics, or escalate failure. - Decision owner: the configured supervisor agent; the built-in arbiter is explicitly the judge role and may use a distinct model/provider. The direct command check is deterministic evidence/support rather than the autonomous judgment owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegate until loop, direct command execution in worker cwd after every turn, no-progress detection, strict verdict parser and worker feedback/retry loop. - Closure path: worker turn → KOT independently executes declared check in worker cwd → failed check output returns to worker, or passing check reaches supervisor → supervisor verdict continue returns specifics to next worker turn, escalate fails, accept permits completion. - Boundary reachability: until and supervisor are standard documented parameters of the shipped delegate operation; arbiter is a built-in agent type, so no downstream reviewer implementation is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic exit-code evaluation supplies complementary operational evidence, while the optional separate supervisor owns the discretionary post-check audit verdict; removing that supervisor removes the independent accept/continue/escalate judgment while leaving the check machinery intact. - Evidence: tools/Agent.md. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: high - Caveats: S3 is established in the supported delegate+until+supervisor mode; ordinary delegates need not exercise it. - Claim being audited: that the delegated worker&#39;s assigned result satisfies the declared completion condition and is ready to be accepted. - Ordinary reporting path: worker turns and the delegate&#39;s eventual task notification/report. - Complementary access path: KOT executes the declared command directly in the worker&#39;s working directory after each turn; a separate supervisor then receives the passing-gate situation and issues a strict verdict. - Independence boundary: the check observes workspace behavior outside the worker&#39;s self-report, and the supervisor is a separately launched agent/judge that can use a different model/provider and has its own tools/context. - Who acts on findings: KOT feeds failed-check or continue findings into the worker&#39;s next turn; escalate fails the loop; only accept closes completion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop is established in the shipped KOT boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external information, prior sessions, persistent notes and available provider/model catalogs can inform current work, but no shipped mechanism closes a future-oriented change to KOT&#39;s organizational capability or governing structure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S4. - Decision owner: none established at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Search, History, Memory, model/provider discovery, user model overrides and durable workflows expand/reconfigure current task capability but do not by themselves own adaptation. - Closure path: no qualifying external/prospective option → adaptation decision → present capability change → subsequent operation loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistent memory and retrieval carry knowledge forward, but they do not establish a distinct external intelligence function with authority to redesign current organizational capability. - Evidence: README.md; tools/Memory.md; tools/History.md; tools/Agent.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity / ultimate-policy closure is established for the KOT organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/provider/settings, user notes, workflow ask gates and host operating rights constrain individual operation but do not constitute an identity/ultimate-policy issue resolved by legitimate ultimate authority and returned into later operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5. - Decision owner: none established at the assessed recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configuration files, provider/model selection, persistent user-scope Memory notes, workflow ask/escalation and ordinary user control of the host process. - Closure path: no qualifying identity/ultimate-policy issue → ultimate authority → authoritative policy decision → returned operational governance loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the lead model can select operational resources and record memory but does not own the ultimate right to redefine KOT&#39;s organizational identity or governing policy. - Evidence: README.md; tools/Memory.md; tools/Agent.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9b6a02efd6a9023ceb7cfe3bf9dedeb380aecb82/assessments/kot.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9b6a02efd6a9023ceb7cfe3bf9dedeb380aecb82\">9b6a02e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-02T22:14:49+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/itissika/litecode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LiteCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-02\">2026-10-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/itissika/litecode/tree/146a9feff9edee2204138f18473b9bef83c41c5a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 146a9feff9edee2204138f18473b9bef83c41c5a\">146a9fe &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing software-engineering work on the selected workspace through open-ended model/tool action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous repository structure, incomplete requirements, implementation alternatives, file contents, command/build/test/LSP results, tool/permission failures, context limits and changing workspace state encountered while completing coding work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which project evidence to inspect, which offered tool/action to invoke, what implementation or command to attempt, how to respond to returned evidence and when the assigned operational outcome is complete. - Decision owner: the model-backed LiteCode primary agent for ordinary work and each separately instantiated model-backed child session for its delegated bounded outcome. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent profiles/tool bindings, model adapters, context pipeline, permission engine, durable session store, snapshots/revert, LSP/search engines, tool pipeline, cancellation and step limits. - Closure path: task plus current workspace/session context → model selects a tool/action → LiteCode executes or rejects it → tool/environment result is persisted into the same session → the model receives the returned evidence and selects the next action or final response. - Boundary reachability: ordinary CLI/server/web/desktop turns instantiate the shipped first-party loop directly; child sessions use the same first-party session/turn primitives rather than an adopter-authored agent runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed actor while retaining tools, state and permission machinery removes the open-ended task-specific software-engineering judgment. - Evidence: README.md; src/agent/core.rs; src/runtime/mod.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider inference is external; the assessment credits LiteCode&#39;s first-party role/tool/feedback composition, not provider internals.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive interference among concurrently executing child coding S1 cells by autonomously assigning non-overlapping write responsibility and reacting when a worker crosses or collides with that boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple mutation-capable child sessions can operate concurrently on one workspace and otherwise overwrite the same files/regions, act on stale overlapping assumptions or create incoherent parallel changes. - Distinct S1 units: separately instantiated general (and user-defined permitted) child sessions, each with its own durable session/context/turn and independent model/tool execution. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent mutation-capable children share the project workspace; overlapping writes or ownership scopes can make one worker invalidate another&#39;s work. - Attenuating coordination relation: the shipped orchestrator prompt requires the manager to choose team structure, who touches which files, who is read-only/writable and to maintain “one writer per file region at a time”; assignments carry explicit constraints/write scope to each child. Process-wide file locks additionally reject simultaneous structured writes/edits to the same file across sessions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the orchestrator&#39;s assignment changes the concrete file scope a child is instructed to touch; live corrections can be sent or the child stopped. If two sessions nevertheless collide through structured write/edit, the blocked tool result names the holder session and tells the affected child to retry after the other writer, so later S1 action changes under the coordination result. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive mapping is tied to an explicit shared-file writer-interference problem and a first-party manager policy plus cross-session exclusion specifically aimed at that interference, not to the mere existence of child sessions, queues or messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the live team/write-scope partition—who may write which region and when a running worker must be corrected or stopped to preserve that partition. - Decision owner: the model-backed shipped orchestrator primary agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagentlaunch responsibility/prompt contract; orchestrator board/write-scope guidance; child role/tool ceilings; process-wide WorkspaceWriteLock; structured resource keys; completion/status/message/stop paths. - Closure path: orchestrator identifies parallel work and interference risk → assigns distinct roles/write scopes in child prompts/board → children act under those scopes → current status/results or a write-lock conflict returns → orchestrator sends correction/stops/reassigns as needed → subsequent child behavior follows the revised coordination. - Boundary reachability: orchestrator is a seeded first-party primary profile with general and explore in its allowed-subagent set and the full subagent tool series bound through the standard runtime; no consumer-authored coordinator is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the process lock is deterministic enforcement, but removing the orchestrator model removes the task-specific decision of how to partition real work/file responsibility and how to revise that relation as current team state changes. - Evidence: src/config/globaldb/builtinprompts.rs; src/config/globaldb/seed.rs; src/tools/subagent/launch.rs; src/tool/writelock.rs; src/tool/executor.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the hard file lock covers structured write/edit, not arbitrary shell-side mutation; S2=A therefore rests on the autonomous orchestrator&#39;s explicit write-scope/interference policy, with the lock as additional enforcement rather than sole evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current team commitments and interventions across the active child-session population on behalf of the user&#39;s present project goal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: children may be running, idle, stopping, completed, failed, blocked, working on obsolete scope or returning evidence that changes which commitment should continue next. - Whole-system current view: subagentlist enumerates every descendant session visible to the primary with agent identity, responsibility, latest assignment preview, raw current session state, running turn age/step and terminal reason; child completions are also injected into the parent through the shared lifecycle source. - Current-control decision scope: choose team size/roles, launch work, inspect the current roster, continue an idle child with a new fitting assignment, send corrections, wait on a fixed result set, stop stuck/known-wrong work and synthesize/reallocate current commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which current child commitments should exist, continue, be corrected, be stopped or be replaced as the user&#39;s active goal evolves. - Decision owner: the model-backed shipped orchestrator primary agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagentlist, subagentsend, subagentstop, subagentwait, durable child-session metadata/status, lifecycle completion router, todo/plan/board files and cancellation. - Closure path: current roster/completion state returns to orchestrator → orchestrator judges current allocation/intervention → launch/send/stop/wait/reassignment action changes active child commitments → later child operation/results reflect that decision and return to the manager. - Boundary reachability: the seeded orchestrator has the standard primary tool bindings and allowed general/explore children. Its shipped system prompt explicitly names team management, roster inspection, continuation and stopping as manager duties. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: session state, lifecycle routing and cancellation only expose/enforce facts. Removing the orchestrator model removes the semantic decision of which current commitments to create, continue, redirect or stop. - Evidence: src/config/globaldb/builtinprompts.rs; src/tools/subagent/list.rs; src/tools/subagent/send.rs; src/tools/subagent/stop.rs; src/tools/subagent/hub.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the human Workers roster is an observation/navigation surface and child panels are deliberately read-only, so no separate parent-governed S3 mode is credited from the UI.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge implementation work through a fresh non-authoring child session assigned to verify specified claims/evidence, then return that verifier&#39;s result to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementation child can report completion while its own assumptions/tests miss defects, or while the manager lacks independent evidence that the delivered change satisfies done criteria. - Claim being audited: that another child/main implementation satisfies the specified coding requirement and verification criteria in the actual workspace. - Ordinary reporting path: the implementing child returns its own conclusion/tests/evidence through its normal durable child turn result. - Complementary access path: the shipped orchestrator policy explicitly requires verification to be “someone who did not write that code” and states that verification/unrelated work should use a new hire/new session; the orchestrator can launch a fresh general child with direct repository/test tools or a fresh read-only explore child for independent inspection. - Independence boundary: the verifier has a separate child session, fresh conversation/context and separately assigned responsibility rather than continuing the implementer&#39;s session. Provider family may be the same, so independence is contextual/access-path rather than model-provider diversity. - Who acts on findings: the orchestrator receives the verifier completion through the parent completion router/durable turn result and can accept, send corrective work, stop/replace a bad implementation path or launch another bounded fix before reporting completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: make the semantic verification judgment from a fresh, non-authoring model context examining the actual project evidence requested by the manager. - Decision owner: the model-backed fresh verifier child selected/assigned by the autonomous orchestrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shipped orchestrator verification rule; seeded general/explore roles; new child-session creation; responsibility/done-criteria prompts; direct read/search/bash/test tool access as role permits; completion routing. - Closure path: implementation result returns → orchestrator assigns explicit proof criteria to a fresh non-authoring child → verifier inspects/runs the relevant evidence and returns judgment → orchestrator uses that finding to accept or change current implementation work → subsequent operation changes accordingly. - Boundary reachability: the verifier is constructed from seeded first-party subagent roles via the ordinary subagentlaunch path, and the requirement for independent authorship is part of the shipped orchestrator prompt rather than consumer-authored coordination code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic session plumbing cannot decide whether arbitrary implementation evidence proves the requirement; removing the fresh verifier model removes the complementary semantic audit judgment. - Evidence: src/config/globaldb/builtinprompts.rs; src/config/globaldb/seed.rs; src/tools/subagent/launch.rs; src/tools/subagent/hub.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: verification is manager-chosen rather than a mandatory gate on every code change; the positive state relies on the explicit non-authoring/fresh-session rule and corrective return, not merely on generic subagent availability.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then loop was established that senses external/future-relevant change, develops adaptation options and returns a selected option into LiteCode&#39;s present organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: session search, context compaction, web-capable explore workers, hot-plug provider/tool configuration, workspace engines and the persistent knowledge base expose information or preserve useful state, but none closes the required prospective organizational adaptation loop in the standard distribution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sessionsearch; websearch/webfetch on explore; project knowledge tool/files; workspace drift reminder; provider/model/agent settings; MCP/custom-tool definitions; code search/LSP readiness; context compaction. - Closure path: not applicable; no first-party external/future distinction → adaptation-option formation → selected persistent capability/strategy change → return into current S3/capability loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistent knowledge is explicitly described as human-owned; agent-created knowledge nodes begin pending and a person enables them. The drift detector tells the agent to notify/ask the user before organizing stale knowledge. Web exploration and session retrieval answer present work rather than autonomously adapting the organization for future environmental change. - Evidence: src/tools/knowledge.rs; src/knowledge/reminder.rs; src/config/globaldb/builtinprompts.rs; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can compose research, knowledge and settings into an adaptation process, but generic composability/current-task research does not donate S4 ownership to the frozen standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: establish and preserve the durable workspace contract that defines project-level standing instructions and advance authorization governing later LiteCode coding behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: inconsistent project rules, missing durable authorization, divergent coding conventions and later agent behavior that no longer reflects the legitimate project owner&#39;s standing constraints. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what persistent project instructions, boundaries and authorization should govern LiteCode agents working in this workspace. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Parent (P) — the legitimate project owner/operator owns the authoritative CLAUDE.md workspace contract. No separate first-party autonomous S5 policy-authoring mode is established. - Return-to-operation path: LiteCode initializes/recognizes root CLAUDE.md; each new turn re-reads it from disk through contractsnapshotforturn; buildsystemprompt splices the contract into every non-hidden agent&#39;s system prompt; later model/tool behavior is therefore governed by the returned owner-selected policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the semantic content of the durable workspace contract, including project instructions and advance authorization boundaries. - Decision owner: the legitimate project owner/operator at the parent recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workspace initialization shell; workspace editor/file persistence; per-turn contract snapshot; system-prompt splicing; permission engine and built-in prompts that explicitly honor durable CLAUDE.md authorization. - Closure path: project policy/authorization issue → owner writes/edits root CLAUDE.md → file persists → the next turn re-reads the fresh contract → LiteCode injects it into the model&#39;s system context → subsequent operation follows that returned standing policy. - Boundary reachability: workspace initialization creates the contract surface in ordinary projects; the standard runtime re-reads it for every turn and the desktop/browser product includes direct workspace editing. No external governance adapter is required for the parent decision to return into operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary agents can technically edit files, but the shipped prompt treats CLAUDE.md as durable user/project authorization rather than an agent-owned self-authorization channel. The decisive ultimate-policy right therefore remains with the legitimate project owner. - Evidence: src/config/workspace.rs; src/contextpipeline/env.rs; src/contextpipeline/system.rs; src/runtime/mod.rs; src/config/globaldb/builtinprompts.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is project-recursion S5, not ownership of LiteCode&#39;s product-level safety model or provider configuration. A user asking an agent to edit the contract does not by itself transfer ultimate authority away from the user.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c496ccc28c34c2726778e33a905ccc589cc832c3/assessments/litecode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c496ccc28c34c2726778e33a905ccc589cc832c3\">c496ccc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T13:30:04Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/crewlet/crewlet\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Crewlet</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/crewlet/crewlet/tree/346d58ee3f3627a7f1213e441c6b0e330ab48030\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 346d58ee3f3627a7f1213e441c6b0e330ab48030\">346d58e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform real organizational work through autonomous per-seat agent turns. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user/work-item requests, external tool state, provider responses, tool errors, knowledge gaps, interruptions, budget limits and results encountered while completing a seat&#39;s assigned work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive tool/action or response after observing current task context and prior tool results. - Decision owner: the model-driven agent seat in the Executor phase. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-seat runner, tool loop, MCP/builtin surfaces, collaboration/skill guards, budgets, delegation guards, inbox/event routing, sandbox suspension/resume and delivery verification. - Closure path: trigger → Executor model decision → first-party tool execution → result appended to the same turn → later model decision → submitted work → reviewer/engine disposition and external result. - Boundary reachability: this is the normal shipped seat execution path; optional external coding agents are not required for the positive S1 path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic engine code constrains and records execution, but the seat model owns substantive next-action choice in the work loop. - Evidence: docs/concepts/agent-runtime.md; docs/concepts/turn-engine.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider-side model internals remain external; credit rests on the first-party closed agent/tool execution path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate conflicting concurrent mutation of shared current work among distinct agent-seat S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two seats can race to change the same native work item, producing incompatible concurrent state if writes are accepted independently. - Distinct S1 units: independently executing Crewlet agent seats operating against the same company&#39;s native tracker. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent writers targeting one work item compete over the same shared work state. - Attenuating coordination relation: the native tracker publishes writes onto the item&#39;s ordered shared log; the broker arbitrates competing writes so two writers racing on one task contend at one ordering point and exactly one wins, while unrelated tasks do not contend. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: write results expose applied, pending, or unknown, nodes replay the same ordered record, and subsequent reads/notifications present the serialized work state back to seats for later action. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation is tied to an explicit same-item concurrent-write conflict and a mechanism specifically preventing divergent acceptance, not to Crewlet&#39;s generic bus, hierarchy, task routing, or delegation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: deterministic shared-log/broker arbitration establishes which competing mutation wins and therefore which state later S1s observe. - Decision owner: no autonomous agent owns the arbitration decision for a concrete write race; the first-party runtime supplies the function-specific deterministic coordination path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ordered native work log, per-object contention, applied-position tracking, operation identity and same-write read-after-apply behavior. - Closure path: concurrent S1 writes → shared ordered arbitration → one canonical ordered state/outcome → replicated tracker state and notifications → later S1 decisions use that state. - Boundary reachability: this path is part of the shipped native tracker backend, one of Crewlet&#39;s documented standard work modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: S2 itself is materially closed first-party, but the collision resolution is deterministic rather than selected by an autonomous coordination actor, so constructor credit is appropriate. - Evidence: docs/concepts/task-engine.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: lead assignment, mailboxes and the generic coordination store are not independently credited as S2; the positive witness is the explicit concurrent shared-work collision path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current team commitments and allocation across active operational seats, with both autonomous lead-agent and human-lead modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new work, blocked work, changing report capacity/fit, parallel task progress and completed/failed results require present-tense assignment, reassignment, review or intervention for the team as a whole. - Whole-system current view: at team recursion the lead receives a roster of direct reports and can inspect the team&#39;s current tracker/work surfaces; assignment and transition notifications return current work changes to the relevant lead/manager. - Current-control decision scope: choose which report receives work, create/assign subtasks, react to blockers and progress, review results, revise current commitments and close/transition parent work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in agent-led mode the lead model reasons over roster/current work and chooses allocation/intervention; in human-led mode the human lead makes the same current-control decisions through the PM/chat surfaces and those changes wake affected agent seats. - Decision owner: autonomous lead agent in the base mode; legitimate human lead in the first-party parent-governed mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: executable org hierarchy, manages/unit lead relationships, lead roster prompt, native/external tracker routing, assignment webhooks, colleague tools, human-seat identity/routing and event delivery. - Closure path: current work/report state → lead decision → tracker/chat assignment or intervention → affected seat wakes/changes current work → progress/transition returns to lead and informs later control. - Boundary reachability: both agent leads and human leads are explicitly supported standard organization modes; human seats can lead units and agent work returns through the same integrated work surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the base task-allocation choice is explicitly described as lead-agent reasoning rather than routing code; the parent mode separately gives that decision right to a human lead and returns the result through first-party routing. - Evidence: docs/concepts/organization-model.md; docs/concepts/task-engine.md; docs/concepts/humans-in-the-org.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a lead title or one-shot delegation is not the witness; credit rests on the documented current-work allocation/review loop and its return through work-state notifications.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge an operational seat&#39;s claim about completed work using a separate reviewer with direct engine-recorded evidence and return the judgment into the same turn&#39;s control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an Executor can claim delivery or quality that its actual tool record does not support, or can produce plausible prose while failing to reach the required external surface. - Claim being audited: whether the round&#39;s work is actually good enough to finish and consistent with what the engine recorded as executed/delivered. - Ordinary reporting path: Executor&#39;s own submitwork outcome, summary, claimed deliveries and produced text. - Complementary access path: a separate Reviewer prompt/model receives the round&#39;s verbatim engine-recorded tool log and work text on a tool surface with no domain tools; engine-side delivery checks additionally compare claims to recorded calls and destinations. - Independence boundary: Reviewer has its own prompt/model phase and narrower tool surface, and the evidence includes engine-recorded calls rather than only the Executor&#39;s self-report. It is therefore materially distinct from ordinary production reporting even though it is routinely invoked. - Who acts on findings: Reviewer can return selfiterate or failed; selfiterate sends the turn back to a fresh Executor round with the prior-work ledger, and engine overrides can also force correction when a claimed delivery did not reach the waiting party. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the reviewer model owns the quality judgment among done, selfiterate, and failed, subject to stricter deterministic delivery truth checks. - Decision owner: autonomous Reviewer model actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: phase-separated prompts/models, forced submitreview, engine-recorded call ledger, delivery Check/OverrideDone, prior-work ledger, stall/iteration guards. - Closure path: Executor result + direct execution record → separate Reviewer judgment → selfiterate/failed/done → next Executor round or termination → later operation reflects the audit finding. - Boundary reachability: Execute→Review is the documented ordinary turn engine, not a development-only evaluator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic checks enforce facts such as whether a write landed, while the separate reviewer model owns the substantive quality/challenge judgment that can send work back. - Evidence: README.md; docs/concepts/turn-engine.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the positive mapping does not rely on a component merely named reviewer; it relies on separate access to engine-recorded reality plus corrective return into execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: derive durable future-operating adaptations from completed work, with an autonomous per-seat learning mode and a parent-governed team-knowledge promotion mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated external work patterns, failures and successful tool trajectories reveal procedures or knowledge that should change how later turns operate; cross-seat convergence can reveal team-level practice worth adopting. - External distinction: learning consumes settled operational turns containing actual tool sequences, review outcomes, counterpart interactions and task results against the company&#39;s work environment rather than only internal planning text. - Future / prospective distinction: synthesized skills, diary/episodes and promoted team practices are retained specifically for future turns; clustered synthesis searches repeated historical trajectories for reusable patterns not needed merely to finish the current turn. - Adaptation option generated: auxiliary-model synthesis drafts reusable per-seat skills; cross-agent promotion distils convergence among distinct seats into a team knowledge draft. - Path back into current capability / S3: synthesized skills are stored per seat and exposed to later turns through the skill catalogue/useskill; a parent-published cross-agent draft becomes an ordinary team knowledge page reachable by future query-time search. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in base mode an auxiliary model can draft/decline a reusable skill and first-party learning stores the admitted result; in parent mode a person/lead decides whether an auto-drafted cross-agent practice is published into live team knowledge. - Decision owner: autonomous auxiliary learning model in the base mode; human/parent reviewer for cross-agent team-practice publication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: post-turn Reflector, episodes, PersistDecider, Synthesizer, duplicate/cap gates, clustered synthesis, Promoter, hidden auto-draft subtree, synthesized-skill store, query/prefetch and useskill/refineskill tools. - Closure path: completed operational evidence → synthesis/proposal → durable skill or parent-reviewed team draft → future prompt/search/tool access → later agent behavior changes. - Boundary reachability: post-turn learning is a shipped engine subsystem; cross-agent promotion is a shipped background duty and publication path, not repository-development learning. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-based synthesis supplies autonomous future-oriented adaptation in the base mode; the separate team-promotion path deliberately holds publication for parent/human judgment rather than silently sharing agent-generated policy. - Evidence: docs/concepts/agent-learning.md; docs/concepts/agent-runtime.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: generic memory or reflection is not the witness; credit rests on evidence-derived reusable skill/practice options with explicit reuse in later operation. The team promotion path remains parent-governed because the draft is hidden until a person publishes it.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: close company identity and ultimate-policy decisions through founder/operator authority and propagate those decisions back into subsequent agent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: questions that change what the company is for or what ultimate policies govern it — mission, vision, company-wide policies and top-level direction — exceed ordinary seat-level task authority. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Tier-B configuration explicitly defines company name, mission, vision and policies; top agents can escalate decisions above their authority to a root human founder whose documented responsibility is to own direction/final calls. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: parent-governed only — the legitimate founder/operator owns Tier-B company configuration through authenticated API/operator credentials; the founder human seat supplies the organizational escalation identity while configuration authority remains the distinct operator hat. - Return-to-operation path: founder/operator decision → versioned PUT/PATCH /config or import of the Tier-B document → activation pointer/control-plane apply across nodes → new epoch → later Executor prompts render the updated mission, vision and full policy text and agents operate under it. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide and authorize company identity/ultimate-policy changes and activate the resulting company revision. - Decision owner: legitimate parent human founder/operator; no autonomous Crewlet agent is shown holding ultimate configuration authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: human founder seat at org root, escalation via normal colleague surfaces, authenticated API tokens, versioned Tier-B config, validation, activation pointer, fleet reconcile/hot reload and per-turn epoch pinning. - Closure path: identity/policy matter reaches founder → founder decides and exercises operator configuration authority → live revision propagates → subsequent agent turns receive changed mission/vision/policies. - Boundary reachability: root human founder seats and live founder-owned Tier-B configuration are both documented first-party deployment surfaces; the docs explicitly distinguish the founder&#39;s colleague seat from the operator credential rather than conflating them. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ultimate identity authority is intentionally human/parent-owned. Static mission/policy text alone would not earn S5; credit comes from the live authenticated parent decision-and-return path. - Evidence: docs/concepts/humans-in-the-org.md; docs/concepts/configuration.md; docs/concepts/agent-runtime.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: ordinary task approval is not S5. The positive claim is restricted to founder-owned live identity/ultimate-policy fields and their explicit propagation into later runtime behavior.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/38590686cb3f3c95b389e5c051cad9a1cf1b6a4c/assessments/crewlet.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 38590686cb3f3c95b389e5c051cad9a1cf1b6a4c\">3859068 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T01:18:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Agent-Field/CodeAF\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CodeAF</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Agent-Field/CodeAF/tree/9258f70523791330bf5128fd5aed6c70a70f138f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9258f70523791330bf5128fd5aed6c70a70f138f\">9258f70 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform and revise scoped user-requested repository/research/tool jobs with model-chosen actions and checked deliveries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous objectives, unexpected repository contents, tool failures, incomplete intermediate outputs and acceptance failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: executing leaf model chooses investigative/tool steps and revised output from its brief and live observations, rather than merely applying a predetermined script. - Decision owner: model-backed worker executing an admitted task leaf, subject to consent, budget and contract gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: exec runner, model/tool adapters, individual work contracts, isolated workspaces, retries and delivery checks. - Closure path: admitted task brief → model action/tool observation → output and receipt → test/contract judgment → repair/replan or delivered result returned to dependent workers and the person. - Boundary reachability: the standard chat graph-runner and do path wire planner output into ordinary executing task leaves; this is not a benchmark-only path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task action/content is selected by the worker model; guards limit its reachable effects without replacing that choice. - Evidence: runtime chat execution, plan builder, resident runner. - Basis: source-grounded standard runtime action-and-feedback loop. - Confidence: high for shipped decision wiring, not for empirical agent success. - Caveats: external model and tool availability was not exercised; bench/ metrics are not production ownership evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: dynamically attenuate incompatible or insufficient inputs between distinct S1 leaves of a shared deliverable, and return the repaired dependency relations to execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: one worker starting without decisions/results from another, duplicate leaves, overly serial dependencies and inconsistent plans under shared deliverable constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-led bind pass judges which upstream outputs each later-stage leaf must await and which duplicate leaf can be folded; a counter-audit can find material missing edges and add them. - Decision owner: model-driven plan binding and counter-audit stages, with deterministic graph validation/enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Graph.Needs, staged fanout, topological stages, Bind, auditWith, AddNeed, Retarget, graph error checks. - Closure path: complete proposed graph → bind model evaluates inter-worker requirements → dependencies materialize → audit model flags missing required inputs → graph adds permissible edges → later leaf sees required predecessor results before acting. - Boundary reachability: both bind and audit are ordinary first-party internal/plan build components, rather than invented orchestration instructions; validated edges are consumed by the graph runner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deciding that a particular other leaf&#39;s answer is required is a contextual model judgment; the DAG and lock code enforce rather than originate that decision. - Distinct S1 units: separate work leaves in a planned multi-stage task graph, potentially executed in parallel within stages. - Inter-S1 disturbance: a downstream implementer missing an earlier design/selection/result; redundant workers or unnecessary serial constraints reducing useful concurrency. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-chosen predecessor edge and duplicate-node fold within one graph, strengthened by opposing finishability audit. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: changed graph Needs determines which upstream outputs appear in an individual leaf&#39;s input and when it may start. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the model evaluates dependencies between independently executable units and alters their information/execution relation; static task fanout alone would not earn this state. - Evidence: binding passes, adversarial dependency audit, graph dependency execution. - Basis: agent-owned contextual inter-S1 dependency regulation with an enforced operational return. - Confidence: moderate-high for the bounded graph coordination function. - Caveats: not evidence of a general independent cross-project resource governor or empirical conflict-free execution; this is one shared deliverable&#39;s graph.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: observe the local installation&#39;s live commitments, commission/cancel/adjust work and return current control decisions into its executing portfolio. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing requests, changed priorities or user corrections, stuck jobs, inadequate scope, interrupted attempts and unseen accumulated commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the agentic head judges when a user utterance requires a new task, a current-job intervention or just an answer, using portfolio-aware reads and actionable tools; in the separate operator mode the person can inspect the same work and direct changes. - Decision owner: model-driven head in base mode (A); legitimate local human owner directs the portfolio in attended control mode (P). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: board/lens/status and toolbelt, durable command journal, reconciler, cancel/replan, bounded tool calls and consent rules. - Closure path: head/owner views live portfolio → selects commission, stop, revision or continuation → journaled command → resident materializes new graph/changes existing work → board and outcomes update for later control. - Boundary reachability: Head.runTurn, task, stop, status and Reconciler.Tick form the standard resident chat path; the attended owner uses the same session/board commands, not an external maintainer issue queue. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tool selection and whether to initiate or redirect a work commitment are model decisions, not automatic queue ticks; user-directed parallel control remains a separate first-party human-owner mode. - Whole-system current view: head&#39;s status() and rendered board unify active jobs, progress, approvals, standing work and outcomes for this installed resident; operator home/TUI exposes the portfolio. - Current-control decision scope: issue a new commitment, withdraw/amend ongoing work or choose next intervention within delegated user instruction and budget. A fixed scheduler cap or time limit alone is not the S3 evidence. - Evidence: head tool-loop and board, commission, portfolio lens, resident application. - Basis: model-chosen whole-resident current control and separate human-override path, both returning commands through the same execution journal. - Confidence: moderate; scoped to this installation rather than company-wide portfolio strategy. - Caveats: model cannot use automatic consent for non-delegated consequential actions; autonomy is bounded by current user authorization.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: independently verify the S3 owner&#39;s current management decisions/claims and return management-level corrective findings through a separate access path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potentially incorrect board-level allocation, intervention and accountability claims; no distinct operational S3 owner found. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for a separate S3-management auditor. - Decision owner: the plan/audit.go model audits inter-task finishability/dependency relations during plan construction; delivery gates audit artifacts, not the head&#39;s portfolio-control judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task gate, graph dependency counter-audit, status visibility and tests. - Closure path: not applicable at the claimed management-audit level; S2 graph audit can correct Needs but does not independently audit S3 decisions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an opposing model prompt is not sufficient if its claim, access and returned finding are about ordinary plan completeness rather than management control. - Evidence: dependency audit&#39;s stated scope, head status path, resident reconciliation. - Basis: bounded negative at local-portfolio recursion after searching plausibly independent plan audit, artifact gate, status and model-head surfaces. - Confidence: medium-high for this source-visible boundary. - Caveats: a different recursion could separately analyze plan quality reviews; this cannot be silently promoted to a whole-organization S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: learn context-specific lessons and reusable work patterns from earlier environmental outcomes, then change how later task units act. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurring external tool/domain surprises, repeated failed approaches, changed recurring preferences and avoidable recreation of earlier work methods. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-driven distillation/reflection judges what a completed job or a series taught; it selects scoped facts/craft proposals for storage or refinement. Optional self-practice selects execution-verifiable gaps under budget/idle rails. - Decision owner: the resident&#39;s configured distiller/reflector model for prospective content and choice, constrained by content validation and scope; deterministic guards admit/store but do not write the strategic lesson. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persisted scoped notebook and recall, NotebookDigest, craft candidate parse/repair/git versioning, retrospective cadence and optional practice metrics. - Closure path: independent job outcomes/corrections → distillation/reflection judgments → recorded notebook fact or validated forged craft → later leaf retrieves scoped notebook input or craft-recognition path → subsequent execution receives changed instructions/workflow. - Boundary reachability: newResidentReconciler wires model distiller/reflector in normal resident mode, and chat.go wires leafNotebookInputs into ordinary worker inputs; durable chat mode wires CraftMind and shelf. One-shot practice is deliberately disabled, so practice is not the decisive S4 evidence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model judges which reusable adaptation to preserve from external outcomes, distinct from a deterministic harvest threshold; the downstream worker consumes it on a later independently scoped task. - External distinction: task results, faults, user corrections, verified failed approaches and changed project conditions supply feedback that earlier model prompts alone could not know. - Future / prospective distinction: lessons and workflows are persisted for future tasks, not merely a repair instruction for the same failing leaf. - Adaptation option generated: scoped operational lesson, reusable preference, replacement belief or executable craft/workflow candidate. - Path back into current capability / S3: notebook retrieval becomes a future worker input; task/charter recognition may activate versioned craft on a subsequent work request. - Evidence: runtime model assembly, resident learning tick, future leaf notebook injection, model craft forge, craft reuse, retrospective. - Basis: a bounded autonomous model-led prospective learning decision and future worker-input closure; not a claim that all designed learning loops work. - Confidence: moderate; structural S4 exists, measured improvement and several secondary feedback paths remain unresolved. - Caveats: docs/LEARNING.md identifies incomplete recall/evidence/router/calibration circuits. No live agent trials were run, and adaptive improvement magnitude is not established.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: determine the standing scope and ultimate limits of what this local installed assistant may do, especially persistent unattended work and consequential tool permissions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user purpose, implicit versus explicit authorization, future requests to widen standing access and risk of unratified autonomous work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the legitimate person explicitly ratifies each standing item and retains power to withdraw, change or bound its budget/permissions; model suggestions are not authoritative creation of standing policy. - Decision owner: local user as parent (P), not the CodeAF model or OSS maintainer organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: standing proposal and approval card, persisted immutable-like rails, permission/consent desk, wake/timer checks, explicit needs your look refusal. - Closure path: user judges proposed standing purpose/limits → ratifies bounded watch/rule → standing store persists authorized item → timer/runner evaluates and executes future firings under those rails → out-of-scope action returns to human instead of self-widening authorization. - Boundary reachability: standard ambient mode uses internal/session ratification and internal/standing storage; cmd/codeaf/wake.go and interactive chat attach the same persistent resident. This is within the installed product, not documentation-only contributor governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model can propose standing purposes, but the authority to install or expand enduring permissions is reserved to the human user. - Evidence: standing store contract, ambient built-path documentation, standing wake assembly, user/session authority. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what enduring purposes, tool rights, spending rails and unattended authority the resident may have on the person&#39;s machine. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: installed human owner (P); no competing first-party autonomous S5 constitution is established. - Return-to-operation path: ratified standing policy in store is consulted by next wake/action; beyond-rail behavior is blocked/presented for a fresh person decision. - Basis: supported positive local parent-authority loop with persistence and enforcement. - Confidence: high for structural ratification/return, without asserting policy compliance under all tool inputs. - Caveats: a user-owned charter is not the same as organization-wide governance of the CodeAF open-source project.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ee34725fb0b79783fb450d026de700d033bb1d62/assessments/co/codeaf.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ee34725fb0b79783fb450d026de700d033bb1d62\">ee34725 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T17:48:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shrec/AIWorkHub\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AIWorkHub</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shrec/AIWorkHub/tree/77bd873460b36df3312090948079d7158ad65081\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 77bd873460b36df3312090948079d7158ad65081\">77bd873 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce a bounded software change and supporting evidence in a managed repository. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task ambiguity, code dependencies, implementation errors and validation observations within allowed writes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose bounded code/tool actions, revise against returned observations and submit a truthful candidate or blocker. - Decision owner: The task-bound worker model actor under the shipped worker runtime contract. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Exact task/claim binding, isolated worktree, provider adapters, Source Graph/context tools, semantic edit and validation MCP surfaces, allowed-write/output enforcement. - Closure path: Manager creates/launches an exact card → first-party worker prompt supplies scope and context → worker reads/edits/tests through bounded tools → observations inform its next actions → exit evidence reaches reviewready → verified acceptance promotes exact bytes or returns residual work. - Boundary reachability: The installed launch MCP tool invokes ProcessManager.launch with the canonical card; buildworkerprompt installs WORKERRUNTIMEPOLICY on the shared launch path and supported CLI/editor adapters expose the worker tools. No AIWorkHub-development-only role is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The worker chooses semantic implementation and repair; machinery neither chooses the patch nor makes its quality claim true. Removing the worker leaves enforced task state but no discretionary implementation. External model/CLI internals are not separately credited. - Evidence: README.md; src/aiworkhub/agenttoolinstructions.py; src/aiworkhub/processlauncher.py; src/aiworkhub/workeraitoolsmcp.py; src/aiworkhub/workerworkspace.py - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Regulate simultaneous write authority and dependency interaction among separate task workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two pending/processing/review cells can own overlapping file/glob scopes, causing destructive code interference or contaminated candidate evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose non-colliding scopes/dependencies and sequence or revise the next work wave from collision/readiness receipts. - Decision owner: Verified manager agent, instructed to plan waves from repository evidence and never parallelize overlapping allowedwrites; deterministic arbitration remains support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan-DAG active write-collision projection, path/glob overlap rules, deterministic priority/task-ID arbitration, launch collision guard, isolated workspaces and retained review scopes. - Closure path: Manager reads taskplansnapshot/collision state → chooses bounded scopes/dependencies and safe launches → launch guard refuses contenders behind active owners → completion/acceptance releases authority → refreshed plan changes which worker may run next. - Boundary reachability: FastMCP installs MCPMANAGERCONTRACTBANNER and managerbootstrap delivers the operating contract; plansnapshot, create/supersede/rework and exact launch tools are available in the initialized manager mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent ownership concerns work-wave/scope choices and adjustment, not the fixed tie-break or lock-like guard. With the manager removed the runtime can retain a reservation and enforce prior cards, but cannot exercise the same scope/wave discretion. - Evidence: src/aiworkhub/core.py; src/aiworkhub/taskplan.py; src/aiworkhub/agenttoolinstructions.py; src/aiworkhub/server.py; tests/testcoretaskplan.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: The source-native overlap/release exercise corroborates support behavior. No multi-model concurrency run or observation of a manager revising an actual conflict is claimed; the ownership finding is structural in the delivered manager mode. - Distinct S1 units: separate exact task-bound coding workers producing different changes in the same managed repository. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concrete overlapping allowedwrites among active pending/processing/review cards; processing and review cells retain scopes. - Attenuating coordination relation: manager chooses non-overlapping waves/scopes or serial dependencies; plan and exact-launch arbitration enforce exclusive admission against active contenders. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: blocked contenders remain unlaunched; accepted/finished owners release scope and the refreshed snapshot makes later work eligible. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the implementation detects exact inter-cell write collisions and governs subsequent write admission, rather than treating a generic DAG edge or mailbox as coordination.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate the managed repository current work portfolio, shared execution capability and accountable acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting present commitments, blocked dependencies, unavailable/costly model routes and unreliable worker completion claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select current task commitments/priorities/scopes and workforce routes; launch, supersede, reject, recover or accept work based on the current whole-repository evidence. - Decision owner: The verified manager agent inside owner-supplied goals, policy and launch/write authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Canonical Plan DAG, health/preflight/workforce catalog/rank, completion inbox, exact identity gates, durable callbacks, cancellation, combined-tree validation and promotion receipts. - Closure path: Manager reads whole-plan readiness/blockers plus live route/outcome evidence → creates prioritized cards and launches safe current waves → callbacks/inbox expose results and failures → manager accepts exact validated deltas or returns residual work/supersedes commitments → canonical state and next wave change. - Boundary reachability: The installed manager bootstrap, Plan DAG, create/launch/supersede/reject/accept tools and callback route form the documented first-task and ongoing-work workflow, usable for any initialized repository. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This goes beyond decomposing one prompt: the contract requires repository-wide current-state reconciliation, work-wave planning and acceptance/intervention over durable ongoing cards. Deterministic ranking and accept gates enforce decisions without inheriting their ownership. Exact token caps remain owner/policy supplied, not autonomously invented by the manager. - Evidence: src/aiworkhub/core.py; src/aiworkhub/server.py; src/aiworkhub/completioninbox.py; src/aiworkhub/workforcecatalog.py; src/aiworkhub/processlauncheracceptreview.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: No discretionary self-granted budget, universal provider availability or autonomous ultimate authority is asserted. Operator read-only dashboards, settings and generic owner prompts do not independently establish a distinct function-complete parent S3 mode. - Whole-system current view: repository task DAG, active/review/blocked states, collisions/dependencies, completion evidence and live workforce availability/outcomes. - Current-control decision scope: current commitments and priorities, safe work-wave/resource route selection, exception recovery, acceptance/rejection and replacement within the owner-defined repository envelope.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Challenge candidate correctness/security/quality against direct operational artifacts independently of the producing worker report. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A reviewready worker may claim success while changed code, callers or combined-tree behavior contradict the claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separate reviewer model inspects the sealed candidate and emits falsifiable lens-specific findings that can prevent acceptance and induce rework. - Decision owner: Separate read-only quality-review agent for the audit judgment; verified manager owns corrective disposition, while receipt parsing/gating is deterministic support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Anti-anchored packet, exact task/request/claim/hash binding, changed source and diff/caller evidence, candidate-scoped Source Graph, readonly reviewer card with empty allowedwrites, authenticated findings and acceptance/refinement gate. - Closure path: After ordinary worker validation, risk-based review launches a separate reviewer on the sealed candidate → reviewer inspects raw evidence without worker rationale → authenticated findings enter acceptance/refinement → blocking findings hold promotion or produce residual rework → corrected candidate must be revalidated/reviewed before exact canonical promotion. - Boundary reachability: Shipped aiworkhubqualityreviewerlaunch and risk-based review orchestration construct/bind the separate read-only card and wire its sealed receipt into acceptreview. This works in supported installed client modes, independently of development CI and the unclosed AuditSystem sink seam. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The credited audit is the distinct blind/raw-evidence reviewer path, not routine worker self-tests or a verifier name. Its semantic judgment is agent-owned; anti-anchoring, isolation and hash checks support independence. Same-provider/model fresh-context mode is explicitly recorded and does not become a cross-provider claim. - Evidence: src/aiworkhub/qualityreviewer.py; src/aiworkhub/qualityreview.py; src/aiworkhub/processlauncher.py; src/aiworkhub/processlauncheracceptreview.py; src/aiworkhub/revieworchestrator.py; docs/QUALITYCONTROL.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Structural reachability and packet invariants are inspected; no live semantic reviewer performance or actual independent-provider run is measured. CAAS naming, deterministic scan findings and ordinary manager review alone are not the positive witness. - Claim being audited: worker reviewready success and semantic correctness/security/maintainability of the exact candidate. - Ordinary reporting path: worker terminal status, self-validation outputs and durable completion evidence. - Complementary access path: independently assembled changed-source/diff/caller and scoped Source Graph evidence; the reviewer may inspect candidate evidence beyond the worker narrative. - Independence boundary: separate sealed, read-only reviewer invocation with no worker rationale/self-verdict and no repository mutation; provider/model separation is an explicitly recorded ladder, not an assumed universal condition. - Who acts on findings: verified manager and review-orchestrator acceptance/refinement path; deterministic gates refuse invalid, missing or blocking review evidence and preserve/rework the candidate.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No supplied function-specific external-and-prospective adaptation loop established at this product boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing external model/tool opportunities or user/environment requirements considered as future capability alternatives, rather than current task defects alone. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Develop and judge future adaptation options from external distinctions and return them into current capability. - Decision owner: No S4-specific autonomous or parent actor/return loop established; managers select current work and internal lessons. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Live workforce readiness and measured-outcome routing; Learning Commit, AI Memory/KB, skill mining/evidence-gated activation/injection, NeedFix/Roadmap and SDLC receipt stages. - Closure path: Lessons from adjudicated task outcomes can become skills and subsequent worker context; model readiness changes current routing. The inspected paths do not supply the external/prospective option-development and capability conversation required for S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Real internal improvement and reusable knowledge are not automatically S4. A generic research task, a roadmap or an agent capable of writing arbitrary future code does not supply that function-specific loop; no closed P alternative is established by owner feature requests. - Evidence: src/aiworkhub/learningcommit.py; src/aiworkhub/skillminer.py; src/aiworkhub/managerskilltools.py; src/aiworkhub/skillregistrystore.py; src/aiworkhub/workforcerouter.py; src/aiworkhub/roadmapstore.py; src/aiworkhub/sdlccasestore.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication and return loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ultimate purpose or unresolved present/future policy tension, distinct from ordinary task acceptance, provider consent and access controls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve an identity/ultimate-policy issue through legitimate ultimate authority and return its decision to govern operation. - Decision owner: Owners/developers configure operating constraints; no function-complete agent or parent S5 path established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Repository identity binding, static repo/provider/quality policies, owner model pins, exact supplied token caps, opt-in write/launch gates and risk/destructive-change confirmations. - Closure path: These settings and confirmations constrain launching, editing and acceptance. The inspected system does not route an identity/ultimate-policy issue for authoritative resolution and apply the returned decision as such. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A manager operating with the owner and static authoritative configuration do not by themselves establish S5. CAAS terminology rules and OSS maintainer policy concern adjacent development/governance, not a supplied installed-product ultimate-policy loop. - Evidence: src/aiworkhub/repopolicy.py; src/aiworkhub/modelsettings.py; src/aiworkhub/core.py; src/aiworkhub/agenttoolinstructions.py; src/aiworkhub/server.py; docs/CAASPROTOCOL.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d321a97560703fdb29eedc1fd3d7328be5c7cc31/assessments/ai/aiworkhub.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d321a97560703fdb29eedc1fd3d7328be5c7cc31\">d321a97 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T18:08:23+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nickthelegend/loom\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Loom</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nickthelegend/loom/tree/01682c8736a130f52e600ef8d351da46a66175a5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 01682c8736a130f52e600ef8d351da46a66175a5\">01682c8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform substantive project coding tasks through mixed worker agents and follow-up corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown implementation requirements, repository state, test errors, differing adapter capabilities and changing user feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: workers choose actual code edits, tests and task-level repair actions within model-driven CLI sessions; coordinator may request another try. - Decision owner: supported external model-backed coding workers composed into Loom&#39;s explicit worktree/adapter operational path; their general internals are not counted as Loom metasystem functions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OrchestraTask worker prompts, adapters, separate task worktrees, run event capture, dependency completion and integration merge. - Closure path: operator goal → model-authored task instruction → worker exploration/edit/test → task result/error/commit → orchestrator feedback and possible follow-up. - Boundary reachability: Orchestra.start creates worker roster and integration worktree; addTask validates agent; schedule starts dependency-ready tasks via runTask and processes completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: implementation steps are not pre-enumerated deterministic scripts; the first-party runner hosts their decisions and records outcomes. - Evidence: pinned orchestra.ts, README and adapters. - Basis: shipped model-agent execution path. - Confidence: high for composition, not for solution quality. - Caveats: external coding models were not executed during this static assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: negotiate and regulate concurrent peer goal/work-unit interference, especially overlapping files and shared project zones in supported Teams mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: separate teammates or tasks editing the same path, hard-zone collisions, dependency races and bounded shared agent capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: dynamically narrow task touches, wait on another goal, or justify proceed: on a soft overlap; hard zone still requires release, while shared lease and limits enforce the decision. - Decision owner: model-backed orchestrator receiving taskheld/status reports and emitting revised spawn actions; constructor-defined coordinator supplies collision facts and blocks hard conflicts, with a separate human stop-wait option. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: path scope/overlap computation, hub-owned leases, hardZones, TeamCoordinator.admit, explicit decide/wait/zone/capacity holds, recheck/lease release, and baton single-writer handoff in local conversation mode. - Closure path: rival lease/file path collision → held task with named holder/conflicting paths → orchestrator&#39;s new overlap/scope/dependency decision → hub re-admission or continuing wait → peer work resumes under enforced scope. - Boundary reachability: the team-linked OrchestraCoordinator is asked before runTask; admitAndRun saves rejected holds, maybeReview returns decide holds to orchestrator, addTask revises pending task scope and overlap. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adaptive overlap response is a model decision, but the actual hub lease, hard-zone prohibition and capacity limit are constructed enforcement; routine worktree isolation alone is not the reason for S2. - Distinct S1 units: independent teammate goals and concurrent task-level worker agents with their own source-editing scope and implementation feedback. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two viable goals may claim overlapping repository paths or a protected hard zone, and one worker&#39;s changes can invalidate or block another&#39;s intended work. - Attenuating coordination relation: the team hub records actual rival leases; model orchestrator can narrow declared paths, wait on another goal or explicitly justify a soft-overlap proceed, while hard zones and caps remain binding. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: held workers do not start; revised work scopes are re-admitted, waiters rebase after another PR merges, and resumed task agents work under updated boundaries. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the reviewed path detects and regulates destructive inter-peer file interference across independent continuing teammate goals, with return to operational schedules, not merely passing work to another agent. - Evidence: pinned team-coordinator.ts, team-leases.ts, orchestra.ts, baton.ts. - Basis: first-party opt-in Teams conflict-decision return path. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: solo/offline Orchestra has worktree isolation but no cross-teammate S2; on soft overlap an agent may choose proceed with a reason, not override protected hard zones.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: control the current goal&#39;s total task portfolio, shared cost/parallel constraints, dependencies, integration and release readiness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled or failed workers, changing dependency conditions, overspend, busy goals, rejected coordination requests and incomplete merge results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: split/assign, reprioritize through dependencies, spawn/revise tasks, send follow-ups, cancel, ask the human and announce done based on whole-goal status. - Decision owner: user-selected model-backed orchestrator of the goal; Loom constructor owns hard execution admission, budget and integration restrictions, including refusing done while tasks remain outstanding. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OrchestraRun all-task state, status report, budget counter, maxParallel, integration branch, dependency scheduler, reviewer/fix results and event log. - Closure path: plan → parallel task results and holds → statusReport containing all work → model&#39;s next action set → new commitments or completed integrated goal; unsettled decisions can pause for owner reply. - Boundary reachability: orchestratorTurn parses actions; applyActions changes run portfolio; maybeReview wakes orchestrator once tasks settle/need decision; runTask/merge update task outcomes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the orchestrator sees and can alter total current work, not only choose the next local tool; creator-set caps condition execution but do not predetermine tactical choices. - Whole-system current view: all tasks, assignment, dependencies, attempts, holds, costs, integration state and project goal within one Orchestra run. - Current-control decision scope: one coding-goal fleet (and team admission), not global business constitutional policy. - Evidence: pinned orchestra.ts, Teams coordinator and README. - Basis: whole-run control with an actual model decision loop. - Confidence: high for supported agent-backed mode. - Caveats: local headless routing without Orchestra does not inherit this planning/control behavior.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the producer/orchestrator&#39;s claim that a delivered code change meets the goal and is safe enough to land. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missed requirements, severe code regressions, deficient tests and producer self-report blind spots. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an auditor from a different model vendor reads actual PR diff and plan in read-only context, issues severity-labelled findings, and returns a pass/fail status that can block landing and reopen corrective work. - Decision owner: separate different-vendor reviewer agent selected from installed agent kinds; Loom parses/severity-gates findings, and the human owner may explicitly override with an auditable reason. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: pickReviewer excludes author vendor, temp review directory, reviewPrompt, parseReview, GitHub comment review, loom/review status, reviewFixPrompt and bounded automatic fix attempts. - Closure path: completed integrated PR/plan → independent raw diff inspection → high-severity findings and status → S3 owner/Orchestra reopening for fixes or human needsHuman → revised head re-reviewed; owner may override with public reason. - Boundary reachability: landing.review calls askAgent(reviewer,tmp,readOnlyPrompt), posts findings and commit status, and invokes orchestra.reopen on unoverridden high findings; Teams review is enabled by default in parsed team policy but opt-in via team-linked delivery. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewer has alternative primary evidence (diff and PLAN.md) relative to task reports, different-vendor execution and a returned corrective path; this exceeds a routine same-agent QA label or passive telemetry. - Claim being audited: integrated producer code and orchestrator completion state are adequate to meet the declared goal without high-severity regressions. - Ordinary reporting path: worker task status/merge results and Orchestra&#39;s aggregate completion report to the goal controller. - Complementary access path: different-vendor reviewer receives actual GitHub PR diff plus plan in a separate read-only temporary workspace and generates severity-labelled findings. - Independence boundary: the selected reviewer&#39;s vendor must differ from all author agent kinds; it cannot modify the producer&#39;s working tree and posts its own PR comment/status; absent eligible vendor is explicitly reported as skipped. - Who acts on findings: the landing controller blocks with loom/review failure and reopens Orchestra&#39;s owner goal for fixes; if the budget is exhausted it asks a human, who alone can log an override reason. - Ordinary report vs audit view: producer reports task completion and integrated branch; auditor reads the actual PR patch and goal plan independently, not only the producer summary. - Independence / access: different installed vendor, isolated temporary work directory and read-only instruction; GitHub status and human override preserve separation from normal producer authority. - Findings / return: severity-labelled PR comment and loom/review check signal trigger fix/reopen or explicit owner override before landing. - Evidence: pinned landing.ts, team-landing.ts, Teams design and policy. - Basis: optional but first-party implemented complementary PR-inspection loop. - Confidence: high for reachable team reviewer mode. - Caveats: if no different-vendor reviewer is installed, review explicitly skips and reports success; a human can override a failure; no blanket guarantee that every install or PR receives an independent audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no clearly owned two-way external/future adaptation program converting environmental changes into prospectively tested new operational capabilities is shipped. - Disturbance / variety regulated: developer tool evolution, future workload mix and team strategy may change, but Loom principally records current turns and shares engineering context rather than constructing future alternatives. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an optional extractor selects durable ADD/UPDATE/FORGET memory records from a turn; that right changes knowledge retrieval, not a prospective adaptation portfolio or redesigned capability selected against future scenarios. - Decision owner: model extractor for memory content; current operator for agent roster/configuration and future integration decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: event-log memory, turn evidence quotes, confidence/untrusted guards, lexical/dense retrieval and downstream agent brief injection. - Closure path: past turn → extracted facts/constraints/failures → persistent brief on later task; no external/future distinction → adaptation hypothesis/options → comparison with current capacity/S3 → selected changed operational capability loop is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: durable memory improves continuity and can influence future local task decisions, but the Profile explicitly distinguishes memory consolidation from S4 prospective environmental intelligence. - Evidence: pinned brain-extract.ts, brain.ts, runtime.ts, README.md. - Basis: scoped negative after examining plausible learning path. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: team/project owners can update policies/tools or agents externally; that manual capability change is not inherited as Loom&#39;s autonomous S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: final mission legitimacy, team identity and ultimate risk policy are not adjudicated by the Loom daemon or its orchestrator. - Disturbance / variety regulated: authorized permissions, team policy, protected repository branches, reviewer/CODEOWNER requirements and spending ceilings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: repository owners and reviewers change loom.team.json on the reviewed default branch and ultimately approve protected changes; ordinary Loom tasks are constrained by that externally settled policy. - Decision owner: external team governance/humans and GitHub repo rules; loadPolicy and stricter are first-party policy transport/enforcement, not ultimate identity adjudication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: origin policy read, local-only tightening, agent permission caps, hard zones, protected branches, signed/posted statuses and owner review/override reason. - Closure path: externally accepted policy file → daemon reads and enforces on subsequent team tasks; no first-party identity-level dilemma → legitimate parent decision solicitation → reviewed mission-policy resolution → return to operation as an explicit internal S5 channel is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewed policy files, user approval and a different-vendor code reviewer are meaningful guardrails but not themselves evidence of a Loom-owned ultimate-purpose decision right. - Evidence: pinned team-policy.ts, landing.ts, Teams design/README. - Basis: function-specific negative for identity authority despite strong governance constraints. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: parent institution may supply an S5 function for its project; this assessment does not transfer its authority to Loom.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6940f3ca4574614f14f1bb7b32f14eace66ba842/assessments/lo/loom.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6940f3ca4574614f14f1bb7b32f14eace66ba842\">6940f3c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T02:21:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DusanCar-sudo/aura-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Aura Code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DusanCar-sudo/aura-code/tree/6fa7733b34ead5458fbf865efe7e2769c57377c3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6fa7733b34ead5458fbf865efe7e2769c57377c3\">6fa7733 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-chosen observation, coding, command execution, verification actions and correction against a task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task uncertainty, changed code, tool failures, unsuccessful test runs, read-only boundaries, provider errors and user steering. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select next tools or delegate a subproblem after observing returned workspace and tool evidence. - Decision owner: lead runAgentLoop model and separately invoked specialist/child model loops for scoped work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registered workspace/search/shell/test tools, permission system, history/context, provider adapter, task budget and user steering. - Closure path: user task → model selects file/search/shell/child operation → tool performs or rejects the action → returned result drives the next model decision and final output. - Boundary reachability: the CLI default path invokes runAgentLoop; spawntask is wired from the run loop to makeDefaultSpawner, and specialist/Archimedes modes instantiate the same model-backed operation loop in shipped paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tooling and permissions are deterministic, but they do not independently decide task-specific actions and remedies without the model. - Evidence: src/agent/loop.ts; src/agent/spawner.ts; src/tools/index.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source and offline mock tests validate routing, not live task success; externally hosted models are inference dependencies, not independently assessed Aurora subsystems.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate cross-specialist dependency, handoff and rejection disturbances among distinct independently acting task workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a reviewer acting before a coder&#39;s prerequisite, competing readiness and failure states, invalid downstream activation after a broken upstream step, and stale previous implementation context after a reviewer requires rework. - Distinct S1 units: model-backed researcher/coder/reviewer/planner task runs with separate prompts, tool limits, result/state identities; parent and spawned subagent loops are also distinct, but their existence alone is not positive S2 evidence. - Inter-S1 disturbance: a reviewer or dependent implementer must consume the right previous implementation status and current artifact; if an upstream coder fails or reviewer blocks its output, starting dependent workers would consume an invalid result. - Attenuating coordination relation: the model-proposed dependsOn DAG guards specialist readiness; the executor propagates failed prerequisites, feeds verified review verdicts back to the coder, resets superseded coder context and re-arms the reviewer. A distinct board path serializes normal writer tasks, although swarm writers remain unguarded. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a blocking model reviewer verdict changes the coder&#39;s status back to waiting; the coder executes again, the reviewer re-runs against that attempt, and later dependent specialists are skipped if review remains blocking. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the cross-S1 feedback changes the admissible subsequent work and causes actual repeat model execution after a conflicting acceptance claim; scheduling/role labeling alone would not do this. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select meaningful specialist dependencies through createPlan, and generate rejection/issue content through a separate reviewer model that forces rework; the executor&#39;s graph rules enforce admissible status transitions. - Decision owner: first-party planning/reviewer model decisions under deterministic DAG/retry limits; protocol board operator independently chooses task ordering in the parent-controlled surface. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: routeTask, createPlan, specialist result records, dependency graph, applyReviewFeedback, propagateSkips, capped reviewer retry and user-board single-writer queue outside swarm. - Closure path: model decides worker graph → workers produce status/artifact → dependency/reviewer findings constrain next work → coder repairs or failed dependencies are skipped → refreshed result returns to execution state. - Boundary reachability: the ordinary CLI may model-route to runOrchestratedTask; --orchestrate forces it; the installed executePlan actually invokes runSpecialist and applyReviewFeedback. The workflow is not hypothetical. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the plan model and independent reviewer choose task-specific dependency/feedback content; deterministic adjacency and retry counters cannot invent what to review or which business task follows. - Evidence: src/orchestration/orchestrator.ts; src/orchestration/executor.ts; tests/orchestration/executor.test.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high, limited to the DAG/review mode. - Caveats: no general cross-file locking was found for all spawntask/swarm workers. swarm.ts explicitly permits concurrent writers to collide, so S2=A must not be interpreted as globally conflict-free multi-agent editing.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: choose and regulate the current set of task workers, dependencies and execution commitments in one active coding organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task complexity needing division, specialist failure, blocked review, unready dependencies, selected agent capacity and current user need to redirect or terminate board runs. - Whole-system current view: the modeled ExecutionPlan retains all specialist steps, dependency/status/result/retry metadata and a synthesis of the active cohort; the board UI/protocol projects actual task/swarm roster and status rather than unrelated GitHub activity. - Current-control decision scope: routing/planning model chooses whether to decompose and which specialists/ordering to deploy; an ordinary lead runAgentLoop can sequentially choose further spawntask delegations after returned child summaries. Authorized board operator selects present tasks/agents, manages order and stops active swarm tiles. - Decisive decision or feedback right: allocate present tasks among workers and, after returned child/reviewer outcomes, continue, retry or terminate work; human parent can explicitly revise current board selection/stop conditions. - Decision owner: model-backed lead/router/planner for active-task division and model-visible follow-up (A); human managing the current board session via protocol (P). Static executor/cap rules constrain but do not own high-level task meaning. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model routeTask and createPlan, executePlan and step report state, spawntask, board-agent presets, protocol board start/stop/move and swarm per-agent status. - Closure path: current user task/perception → planning model selects a worker graph and executes it → reported specialist outcome or reviewer block updates current team state and lead synthesis/follow-on model choice; in parent mode, live board state → human task/roster choice or stop → protocol changes current runs and broadcasts updated status. - Boundary reachability: the standard CLI uses model routing before the solo path and supports explicit orchestration, while the shipped server protocol runs task/swarm agents and responds to task transitions. Current board run uses actual runAgentLoop rather than dummy labels. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model selects task-specific division of labour; the operator makes discretionary choices for the active board rather than merely setting fixed startup flags. The host alone would only enforce queues and render status. - Evidence: src/cli/index.ts; src/orchestration/router.ts; src/orchestration/executor.ts; src/protocol/handler.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: the model creates the specialist DAG up front; within its run, many transitions are deterministic and the plan is not continuously reauthored by a persistent executive model. Board human control is for the present task/roster; it does not authorize cross-workspace global governance.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a coder model&#39;s implementation claim with read-only reviewer/model inspection and require bounded correction if the verdict blocks; optional host-side verification supplements this. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementer declares success despite missed requirements, incorrect code, under-tested changes or conflicting evidence; self-report alone is not sufficient in the orchestrated reviewer branch. - Claim being audited: a completed coder specialist step and its artifact/result, distinct from the evaluator&#39;s later read-only review; the optional --verify wrapper separately tests ordinary loop claims against changed files/tests. - Ordinary reporting path: coder runAgentLoop terminal summary, tool history, stored step result and done status. - Complementary access path: reviewer is dispatched as its own runAgentLoop with REVIEWERSYSTEMPROMPT, a fresh task brief with dependency memory and enforced read-only permissions; it can inspect repository facts. The independent model&#39;s structured issues/blocking verdict, rather than coder prose, enters parseReviewVerdict. The --verify wrapper separately re-reads filesystem state and optionally re-runs configured tests. - Independence boundary: separate run, reviewer role, read-only capability and independent verdict decision, but the configured provider can be the same vendor/model and the reviewer depends partly on coder-provided summaries. The host review parser treats unparseable output as nonblocking; a caller must include a reviewer step for this active path. These limits bound, but do not erase, model ownership of the configured first-party review mode. - Who acts on findings: executePlan.applyReviewFeedback reopens dependent coder step and resets superseded memory, then executes a fresh review. Persistent blocking at the one-retry limit marks review failed and skips dependents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose specific acceptance/rejection judgment from fresh read-only inspection after the implementer&#39;s claim, with authority to block or reopen the worker&#39;s current task through the reviewed plan. - Decision owner: independently invoked model-backed reviewer specialist, with engine-owned parsing, dependency and retry guard; deterministic --verify is only supplemental constructor evidence, not the basis for agent-owned review judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runReviewer, specialized prompt, read-only tool permissions, structured findings, parseReviewVerdict, capped applyReviewFeedback, runWithVerification file/test checks. - Closure path: coder claims completion → separate reviewer examines task/code and emits structured verdict → blocking issue resets coder and reviewer → coder revises, reviewer checks again or failure prevents downstream promotion. - Boundary reachability: --orchestrate invokes executePlan; createPlan supports a reviewer PlanStep with dependency edges and the executor dispatches runReviewer through a shipped first-party path. Offline tests cover one-retry, blocking and skip behavior. A normal solo/Archimedes task need not enter this optional reviewer path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: acceptance issues and blocking are substantively selected by a distinct reviewing model, whereas the host would otherwise only have fixed file/test checks; removing reviewer model choice leaves no equivalent semantic review verdict. - Evidence: src/orchestration/specialists.ts; src/orchestration/executor.ts; src/orchestration/specialist-prompts.ts; tests/orchestration/executor.test.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for wired optional audit, not for real-world detection effectiveness. - Caveats: parseReviewVerdict intentionally treats unparsable results as nonblocking and explicit blocking:false as authoritative even if severity is critical; review cannot be claimed as fail-closed in all cases. User-selected reviewers/plan quality and same-model-provider self-critique can reduce substantive independence; no live LLM results measured.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: adapt future inference-capacity selection to measured competence of the local model on similar past tasks and periodically retest its capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: local model capability differs by task pattern, untested or degraded model performance threatens correctness and spend, and fixed routing rules can keep competence estimates stale as local models evolve. - External distinction: recorded attempts reflect actual user tasks, downstream independent-verifier judgments and availability of a separate local inference endpoint. These outcomes are environmental performance evidence beyond the internal current-token prediction. - Future / prospective distinction: saved episodes are reloaded on subsequent tasks; their task-similarity success rates change whether future tasks attempt the local model or go straight to the large model. Epsilon probes revisit previously gated patterns to detect competence changes. - Adaptation option generated: assessCompetence produces a future-task useArchimedes policy decision from similarity/threshold/minimum evidence, with a low-rate probe alternative; resolveArchimedesConfig also selects a currently available local backend/model. - Path back into current capability / S3: the chosen branch of ArchimedesAlternator.run actually instantiates a local read-only agent or a large-model agent, independently verifies the local answer, escalates if invalid, records a durable Episode, and the next invocation reads those episodes to revise the policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which model-capacity tier is trusted for a similar upcoming task and when to re-probe, then feed the observed success outcome back into future routing. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic competence matcher/threshold/epsilon controller and explicit operator configuration; no autonomous model owns broader future organizational strategy or open-ended capability redesign. Thus a substantive adaptive constructor (C) rather than A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: episode store, task similarity and competence assessor, local endpoint probe, read-only local task loop, second verifier call, escalation to large model, cost log and epsilon re-probe. - Closure path: real task → local attempt and separate verification → persisted success/failure episode → subsequent similar-task competence estimate → alternative model-tier decision and occasional probe → updated operating capacity/cost and new evidence. - Boundary reachability: CLI and REPL explicitly compose ArchimedesAlternator when enabled and local model available; its actual run body loads and saves Episodes and applies routing decisions. It is an optional mode and is skipped in the --verify branch of the same CLI dispatch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model outputs are used as solver/verifier signals, but the prospective routing strategy is a fixed code-defined threshold/epsilon decision, not an autonomous S4 agent inventing and promoting strategic alternatives. - Evidence: src/cli/index.ts; src/archimedes/alternator.ts; src/archimedes/competence.ts; tests/archimedes/competence.test.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for conditional wiring, lower for operational generalization beyond similar task strings. - Caveats: this is bounded future capacity routing, not autonomously improved model weights, online fine-tuning, organizational scenario forecasting, or self-modifying production code. --propose-harness uses a hardcoded patch registry and the default apply path targets the Aura source checkout rather than an arbitrary installed user runtime; it is not evidence of a broader autonomous S4.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct installed ultimate-purpose/identity-policy decision loop returning a binding constitutional ruling to the same coding organization was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting tool permissions, provider trust, read-only reviewer limits, operator instruction and persistent persona preferences are operating constraints or context, not organizational existential policy questions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: absent at the assessed recursion; users choose task, capability settings and desired board work, while developers supply fixed prompts/permission policy. - Decision owner: no first-party S5 adjudicator; ordinary user/developer configurations or saved identity text are external inputs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission roles, AGENTPRESETS, safety confirmations, managed access to board, project memory and persona/identity context. - Closure path: requested action → configured permission/role check → operation admitted or blocked; no identity/purpose tension → legitimate final authority → returned governing-policy change is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-operation safety, model-rank trust, long-term memory and human ability to terminate a task do not by themselves instantiate an ultimate organizational policy function. - Evidence: src/safety/permissions.ts; src/board/agents.ts; src/agent/unified-memory.ts. - Basis: bounded structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: user&#39;s global moral/legal authority over their own computer is not a separately wired in-product identity-policy return process at the coding-session recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/697a8ee229822d9a91afe386bd2303158e9528a4/assessments/au/aura-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 697a8ee229822d9a91afe386bd2303158e9528a4\">697a8ee &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T18:19:49+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Wholiver/metis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Metis</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Wholiver/metis/tree/8dc0d6da31e878b620ef3c3a412a5c7dff8164a2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8dc0d6da31e878b620ef3c3a412a5c7dff8164a2\">8dc0d6d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously inspect, modify and verify software through model-selected repository/tool actions, directly at root or in bounded implementation agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar code, implementation decisions, build/test/runtime failures, tool/process errors, context pressure, model/provider variability and bounded delegated work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive engineering actions, interpret repository/test evidence, select repairs and decide when an assigned operational objective is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed root coding agent and write-capable implementer agents within their admitted boundaries. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool runtime, permissions, model routing, sessions/compaction, spawn guard, worktrees, Performance lane contracts and verification tools. - Closure path: user/current parent objective → model chooses direct or delegated implementation actions → first-party runtime executes and returns evidence → agent revises/repairs → operational result returns to parent or closes the root task. - Boundary reachability: ordinary and governed Performance modes instantiate the shipped AgentSession/tool runtime; implementer is a built-in named agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic host machinery constrains execution, but the model chooses and interprets the open-ended engineering work. - Evidence: README.md; src/core/agent-session.ts; src/core/agent-definition.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: T0/T1 may remain single-root operation; positive multi-agent functions are claimed only for reachable governed modes that instantiate them.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive interference among concurrently active coding S1 lanes by choosing dependency-safe ownership boundaries and enforcing non-overlapping mutation/isolation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent agents editing overlapping paths, shared mutable-state collisions, integration drift and dependency-order violations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose operational lane decomposition, dependencies and owned paths, determine which lanes may run in parallel, and select isolated versus shared execution under admitted runtime policy. - Decision owner: the model-backed coordinator/root operating from the typed Performance admission and current roadmap. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: T3 disjoint-owned-path admission checks, SharedMutatingOwnerRegistry overlap rejection, worktree snapshot/isolation, SpawnGuard concurrency/depth caps and child workspace cleanup/retention. - Closure path: coordinator decomposes current work into owned lanes → runtime checks dependencies/overlap and creates isolated workspaces where required → peer workers execute without shared mutation → their results/integrated workspace return → coordinator proceeds with integration/assurance or repairs rejected lanes. - Boundary reachability: built-in coordinator and Performance T2/T3 routes are shipped; \\spawnagent\\ exposes lane/worktree bindings and the runtime enforces admitted ownership. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime rejects illegal overlap, but the coordinator owns the discretionary task partition, lane boundaries and parallelization decisions that define the coordination relation. - Distinct S1 units: root/implementer coding agents operating admitted implementation lanes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping writes/shared mutable state can corrupt or invalidate parallel work; frozen Performance admission explicitly rejects overlapping T3 lane paths and shared mutable state. - Attenuating coordination relation: dependency/owned-path partition plus physical worktree isolation or shared-cwd exclusive mutation claims. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: conflicting ownership prevents launch/parallelization; child results and integrated state determine whether lanes can advance to assurance or require repair. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanism is explicitly tied to a concrete peer-S1 mutation/interference risk and changes whether/how those peer units may execute. - Evidence: src/core/agent-definition.ts; src/core/performance-runtime.ts; src/core/tools/spawnagent.ts; src/core/workspace-probe.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: worktree/ownership enforcement is deterministic; the A classification rests on the model coordinator&#39;s discretionary decomposition and parallelization authority.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a whole-current view of delegated work and regulate active commitments through dispatch, wait, guidance, termination, backtracking and gate/frontier decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failed, blocked, timed-out or runaway workers; incomplete dependencies; reviewer/verifier rejection; active-child capacity; lanes that need repair or replanning. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the current worker set/status/results, message or kill a selected worker, wait/collect groups, decide which dependency-ready lane to dispatch, stop completed workers and route failed assurance back to implementation/planning. - Decision owner: the model-backed root/coordinator in reachable multi-agent Performance modes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: \\listagents\\, \\waitagent\\, \\killagent\\, \\messageagent\\, SpawnGuard lifecycle state, Performance frontier/gates, child result schema and process-group cleanup. - Closure path: root/coordinator observes current frontier plus child statuses/results → makes a current commitment/control decision (dispatch/wait/message/kill/backtrack/advance) → runtime executes it → updated worker/frontier evidence returns → parent makes the next current decision. - Boundary reachability: agent-management tools are first-party model-callable tools; the built-in coordinator prompt explicitly requires current worker lifecycle management and zero live children at handoff. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic lifecycle/state machinery exposes and enforces controls, but the coordinator chooses the substantive response to current worker/gate state. - Whole-system current view: Performance state supplies current frontier/admission/lane state while \\listagents\\ exposes tracked children, status, parent/depth, elapsed time, exit/result/error. - Current-control decision scope: current lane dispatch, collection, targeted guidance, kill/cascade, retry/backtrack after failed assurance and final convergence/handoff. - Evidence: src/core/tools/agent-management.ts; src/core/agent-definition.ts; src/core/performance-runtime.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: hard concurrency/depth/timeout limits themselves are not S3 owners; they support the coordinator&#39;s live current-control decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge implementation claims through author-independent review and grounded verification before the Performance frontier can converge. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect implementation claims, scope creep, security/regression risks, missing tests and false completion despite failing real-system evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently inspect the integrated repository/diff or run real verification and return pass/reject/failure evidence that determines whether work may advance. - Decision owner: separate built-in reviewer and verifier/fresh-verifier model agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated read-only/restricted role toolsets, fresh child contexts, G5/G6 Performance gates, real repository/test access, typed child results and backtracking rules. - Closure path: implementation reaches assurance frontier → root/coordinator dispatches fresh reviewer and verifier → those agents independently inspect integrated code/run evidence → verdicts return to host → failures backtrack to implementer/planner; passing assurance permits frontier advance. - Boundary reachability: reviewer/verifier are built-in roles and explicitly admitted by Performance routes; frozen runtime maps reviewer to G5 and verifier/fresh-verifier to G6. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: substantive audit/verifier judgments come from separate model contexts with direct repository/runner access; the host gate only validates/routes their result. - Claim being audited: implementation correctness/completeness and readiness of the integrated change. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer&#39;s own ChildResult and implementation/test account. - Complementary access path: reviewer directly reads/searches/diffs the repository; verifier executes grounded tests/typechecks/runtime checks against the integrated workspace. - Independence boundary: author-independent named roles, fresh child processes/contexts, role-specific tools and explicit G5/G6 assurance gates. - Who acts on findings: root/coordinator/Performance host routes failures back to implementation/planning and only advances on passing assurance. - Evidence: src/core/agent-definition.ts; src/core/performance-mode.ts; src/core/performance-runtime.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: host-side deterministic gate validation is support; S3 ownership rests on the independent reviewer/verifier judgments and evidence paths.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established at the assessed frozen revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying changing outside/future condition is shown being transformed by a distinct intelligence owner into adaptation options that return into current S3 capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SQLite memory extraction, searchable durable records, memory map/overview, session resume, provider/model configuration and extensions preserve/retrieve experience but do not by themselves close S4. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the memory coordinator extracts and stores procedures/failures/preferences and can inject/retrieve them later, but the frozen \\src/core\\ exposes no separate adaptation mechanism that converts external/future distinctions into prospective organizational options returned to current control. - Evidence: src/core/memory-coordinator.ts; src/core/tools/query-memory-db.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: later repository revisions may add stronger self-learning/adaptation machinery; that cannot be backported into this frozen review.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level or ultimate-policy dispute is routed to an authoritative S5 owner and returned as governing organizational policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Performance admission, system prompts, role/tool allowlists, trust/security settings and user/project configuration strongly govern execution but operate below identity/ultimate-policy closure. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: root/coordinator can govern task execution and assurance but cannot authoritatively redefine Metis&#39;s identity or ultimate operating principles. - Evidence: src/core/system-prompt.ts; src/core/performance-runtime.ts; src/core/agent-definition.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: strong execution governance is not automatically S5; no ultimate identity-policy issue/authority/return path is established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2c9457de7d646150ddf96fb189e2b28a953a8fca/assessments/metis.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2c9457de7d646150ddf96fb189e2b28a953a8fca\">2c9457d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T21:40:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Sir-chawakorn/sanook-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sanook CLI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Sir-chawakorn/sanook-cli/tree/c7bbede1823b68f4c48959dc34d864a5404762c8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c7bbede1823b68f4c48959dc34d864a5404762c8\">c7bbede &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: handle user coding and assistant requests by selecting and executing tools, examining failures and iterating; optionally delegate independently executed subtasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varying user goals, source/test feedback, command failures, retrieved context, tool permission outcomes and provider-generated uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the primary and child models select tool calls and subsequent operations from messages/results; the runtime executes and reports those calls. - Decision owner: configured model-backed runAgent instance, with child runAgent loops as separately deciding S1 units. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: streamText provider adapter, tool schemas, budgets, permissions, context assembly, local files/Git/web tools and child context isolation. - Closure path: task/context → model tool choice → tool result or refusal → next model turn / delegated child / completion. - Boundary reachability: CLI/headless/gateway handlers call runAgent; exposed task tools import and call the same loop for each child, in the installed product. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model selects substantive task execution and repairs, whereas the TS runtime and policy gates only transport/enforce the chosen steps. - Evidence: loop, CLI, task, mock loop test. - Basis: frozen source, static call wiring and native mock tests; no live inference. - Confidence: high in executable agent constructor, not in model effectiveness. - Caveats: real providers, external services and interactive quality were not tested; the selected integration suite had environment-sensitive failures.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent independent concurrently writing child S1 units from corrupting the same project working tree, and return integration collisions to a coordinating parent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: overlapping edits and incompatible file changes produced by parallel model-backed children. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the primary model can choose taskparallel with isolate=true for a particular set of writers; after separate execution, first-party patch application reports whether each result was integrated, so the parent can change the next task/repair. - Decision owner: the primary model for whether and how to isolate the delegated batch and subsequent response to reported conflicts; deterministic worktree logic for enforcement and sequential merge order. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: private Git worktrees, child-local cwd/async contexts, diff capture, git apply --check --3way, sequential apply and failure reporting. - Closure path: model classifies potentially overlapping parallel work → selects isolated batch → separate children operate on private trees → diffs applied in a fixed order → merge/conflict notes return as parent-model tool output → later operational decisions can change. - Boundary reachability: taskParallelTool directly calls runIsolated when the model chooses isolate; runIsolated invokes runInWorktrees, and the registered parent agent can consume its merged outcome. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the discretionary choice to use isolation and what to do with collisions belongs to the model, not to a fixed supervisor. This supports bounded A for the explicitly isolated batch, not universal automatic coordination; the patch merge itself is deterministic and semantic conflict resolution is not proven. - Evidence: parallel and isolation tool, worktree, offline tests. - Basis: executable first-party interference guard and tests with injected fake child work. - Confidence: medium-high for the optional interference-specific path; no live model choice measured. - Caveats: the default non-isolated parallel path leaves writers sharing a workspace; isolate must be selected, and patch conflicts are reported for later resolution rather than autonomously semantically merged. - Distinct S1 units: two or more independent model-backed child runAgent loops with writable toolsets and separate contexts. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent writes to overlapping source paths and contradictory proposed edits of the shared project. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-selected isolate=true creates a separate Git worktree for each writer, then serially checks/applies returned diffs. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: each isolated task returns success/failure and patch conflict notes, allowing the primary model to choose follow-up work after the batch. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: dedicated code isolates an actual multi-writer conflict class and explicitly checks integration collisions, changing the shared filesystem&#39;s interference topology.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a whole-session picture of currently running background child commitments and selectively stop work that is no longer justified. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unnecessary, prolonged or mistaken active child commitments consuming bounded task slots and attention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the parent model obtains the entire TaskRegistry via taskstatus, selects a running child id and calls taskcancel when current commitments should change. - Decision owner: parent model for selection/intervention; registry AbortController for termination enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: process-scoped registry with states, start/end timestamps, taskspawn, taskcollect, taskstatus, taskcancel, shared budgets and abort signals. - Closure path: current child roster → parent judgment → targeted taskcancel → child abort/status update → subsequent model status/collection and reprioritized work. - Boundary reachability: all four tools are in the registered parent tools map, and TaskRegistry survives successive tool calls within one ordinary session; model-facing instructions advertise these control actions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: choosing which of all current child commitments to stop is a discretionary parent-model decision; a fixed concurrency ceiling or abort API by itself would not establish S3. - Evidence: task status/cancel, registry, native task tests. - Basis: tool and registry wiring, selected offline tests. - Confidence: medium-high for the narrow session roster/intervention circuit. - Caveats: no demonstrated model optimization of priorities or resources beyond selective background-task cancellation; TUI tasks overlay lists and inspects but has no direct selective-stop operation, so parent mode is not claimed. - Whole-system current view: taskstatus lists every retained background job in the local session with id, state, duration and description. - Current-control decision scope: the parent model can terminate a selected currently running child via taskcancel and reconsider subsequent commitments; it does not govern external agents or separate installations.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no materially complementary, independently deciding audit of the coding organization was established in the supported first-party session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: undetected errors could remain after ordinary tests, child reports and memory retrieval. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct auditor is first-party wired to acquire independent evidence, judge an operational claim and return an audit finding to a corrective control path. - Decision owner: the same primary agent can request ordinary child research/review, and the user can inspect reports; neither constitutes a shipped complementary audit body. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generic task/taskparallel, code tests, memory health brain-doctor/brain-eval and TUI status information. - Closure path: task and test outputs re-enter ordinary S1 processing; no separate first-party independent audit→finding→control return is defined. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic fresh child delegation does not supply an explicitly distinct audit judgment, access policy or substantive independence, and deterministic memory metrics are not an agent-owned complement to operational control. - Evidence: task delegation, brain evaluation, CLI. - Basis: first-party child tools and native diagnostic routes inspected. - Confidence: medium-high within the supported local boundary. - Caveats: an operator could manually prompt a separate subagent for review; that external composition is not claimed as shipped S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: alter the future repertoire of the locally installed agent by generalizing successful recurring procedures into reusable skills. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future recurring task types and the cost/error risk of reconstructing a multi-step procedure from scratch every time. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the primary model decides after a successful multi-step task whether the procedure is likely to recur, invents a general runbook and chooses to call createskill; the operator permission gate may approve/deny persistence in ask mode. - Decision owner: primary model in the first-party supported automatic-write mode for prospective skill selection/content; deterministic store for saving and making it available on later tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model system instruction about reusable successful work, createskill schema, slug-validated global skill files, loadSkills, findskills and skill retrieval into new session prompt context. - Closure path: observed completed work plus inferred future repetition → model generalizes/chooses a new capability → saveSkill persists it → later runAgent loads its description and can follow the body → subsequent operating repertoire changes. - Boundary reachability: the primary runAgent registers createskill, instruction explicitly requests prospective reusable skill creation and the supported --yes/automatic permission mode permits persistence; new sessions invoke loadSkills and expose saved skills to their models. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the prospective choice of what future capability is worth installing and its procedural content belongs to the model; fixed skill loading, saved memory and cache are infrastructure rather than the adaptation decision itself. - Evidence: agent future-use instruction, skill creation, skill store/reload, offline roundtrip. - Basis: explicit longitudinal future-use instruction, model-selectable durable capability writer and deterministic roundtrip tests. - Confidence: medium for bounded capability adaptation; no live-model autonomous learning outcome was measured. - Caveats: prospective signal is inferred expected task recurrence rather than a broad external market/environment scan; the default ask permission mode may require an operator approval, and no skill-quality improvement was empirically demonstrated. - External distinction: a user/project-originated task and its execution outcomes reveal a repeatable work pattern beyond the agent&#39;s built-in procedure repertoire. - Future / prospective distinction: the primary model judges after a successful multi-step execution that the same type of procedure is likely to recur in later tasks. - Adaptation option generated: the model decides whether to codify an additional skill and chooses its name, applicable conditions and procedural body. - Path back into current capability / S3: saveSkill installs the global capability; later loadSkills advertises it to subsequent runAgent sessions and skill retrieves its steps for future S1 execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no governing identity or ultimate-policy institution with its own issue/decision/return loop is established for a local Sanook installation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing purpose, legitimacy and fundamental organizational tradeoffs are outside the shipped current-task/permission circuits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S5 body arbitrates ultimate purpose or identity and binds subsequent lower-system choices; the user chooses tactical permission/configuration. - Decision owner: operator for per-tool approval and model/config settings, upstream maintainers for product direction outside the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ask/auto tool gating, trust scope, personality and goals configuration, environment secrets and gateway access boundaries. - Closure path: approvals and configuration affect immediate tool execution; no complete identity-policy question → ultimate legitimate decision → returned governing constraint is present. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: permission enforcement and persona injection do not constitute an organization-level ultimate-policy authority. - Evidence: approval gate, CLI config, agent prompt. - Basis: inspected policy, configuration, identity hints and installed operating paths. - Confidence: medium-high for the local session boundary. - Caveats: legitimate human oversight exists but is principally tactical tool approval, not proven S5 parent governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a615d3ab6a0222038cd098c2da463f946585b387/assessments/sa/sanook-cli.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a615d3ab6a0222038cd098c2da463f946585b387\">a615d3a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T23:12:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/omnigent-ai/omnigent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Omnigent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/omnigent-ai/omnigent/tree/4d963a360e798f076d4fdbd4665e7188e4da05df\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4d963a360e798f076d4fdbd4665e7188e4da05df\">4d963a3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. First-party agents and supported external coding-agent backends autonomously perform model/tool work inside Omnigent sessions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: A. Polly closes a concrete coordination loop across parallel workers: it decomposes work, assigns independent worktrees, tracks task dependencies/results, reacts to failures and re-dispatches work. Worktree isolation attenuates file collisions while task-state feedback changes later worker behavior.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: A. The autonomous Polly orchestrator has a whole-run view and authority over assignment, worker/model selection, cancellation, failure recovery and re-dispatch. It therefore regulates current team commitments rather than merely forwarding tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: A. A separate cross-vendor reviewer receives the implementation diff and contract rather than the implementer&#39;s working context, independently judges the result, and blocking findings are converted automatically into corrective fix tasks before human merge. This supplies complementary access plus closed corrective feedback.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. No distinct outside-looking prospective adaptation loop that develops future organizational capability was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. ALLOW/DENY/ASK, budgets, sandbox rules and human escalation constrain execution. They do not establish an identity/ultimate-policy tension resolved by legitimate S5 authority and returned as changed organizational policy.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/aa0f4fc244d04c276678f55ed941376422a344af/assessments/omnigent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit aa0f4fc244d04c276678f55ed941376422a344af\">aa0f4fc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-02T21:44:11+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/matrixheaven/neo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Neo</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-02\">2026-10-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/matrixheaven/neo/tree/2828e253852a98cace95dc404caf3ac8a3e45bce\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2828e253852a98cace95dc404caf3ac8a3e45bce\">2828e25 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing software-engineering work on the selected project through open-ended model/tool action, including repository inspection, coding, shell/tool execution and task-specific response to returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous source trees, incomplete requirements, implementation alternatives, changing workspace state, tool/build/test failures, provider responses, permissions, project instructions and local execution results encountered while completing coding outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose what project evidence to inspect, which offered coding/tool action to invoke, what implementation change or command to attempt, how to react to tool/environment results and when the assigned coding outcome is ready. - Decision owner: the model-backed Neo main agent for ordinary work and each separately instantiated model-backed coding child for its delegated operational outcome. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, permission layer, project instruction engine, provider adapters, session/context state, process supervision, worktree binding, role tool ceilings, context compaction, cancellation and task/workflow persistence. - Closure path: task plus project/session state enters a model-backed agent → the agent chooses a repository/tool action → Neo executes or rejects it → environment/tool result returns into that agent context → the agent revises the next action until a terminal outcome. - Boundary reachability: interactive and headless Neo execution directly instantiate the first-party model/tool runtime; Delegate, DelegateSwarm and workflow child execution instantiate the same first-party child runtime without adopter-authored agent loops. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed actor while retaining tools, permissions, journals and schedulers removes the open-ended task-specific coding judgment that chooses and revises operations. - Evidence: README.md; turnloop.rs; delegate.rs; runtime.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider/model inference remains external; the assessment credits Neo&#39;s first-party role/tool/feedback composition and not provider internals.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate destructive workspace interference among distinct mutation-capable child S1 cells by assigning independent Git worktree execution roots before those cells act. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two independently executing coding children working from the same checkout can overwrite or observe one another&#39;s in-progress changes, couple through stale workspace assumptions and lose clean ownership of separate change slices. - Distinct S1 units: workflow-hosted coder children are separately instantiated model-backed AgentRuntime units with their own lifecycle, task/context and tool execution. - Inter-S1 disturbance: mutation-capable slices of one refactor can otherwise interfere through a shared checkout even when each slice is locally reasonable. - Attenuating coordination relation: workflow child plans expose ChildWorktreePolicy::Isolated; resolvechildworktree creates a dedicated detached Git worktree before child start; the shipped large-refactor workflow explicitly requests isolated worktrees for its mutation slices and never auto-merges or deletes them. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the resolved isolation changes the actual workspace root supplied to each child before execution, so later reads/writes occur in distinct checkouts; merge/retirement remains an explicit later decision instead of allowing concurrent slices to destructively share one mutable root. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation is tied to a concrete shared-workspace mutation conflict and the first-party worktree separation specifically intended to attenuate that conflict. Delegate mailboxes, queues and lifecycle state are not independently used as S2 evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: bind a mutation-capable operational child to an isolated worktree rather than the shared parent workspace when constructing the multi-child workflow. - Decision owner: constructor-level workflow definition/authoring selects the isolation policy; the runtime deterministically resolves and enforces it. No shipped autonomous coordinator is evidenced as semantically deciding, from observed collision risk, which concrete child pair must be isolated. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ChildPlan and ChildWorktreePolicy; workflow child isolation resolver; WorktreeManager; typed child workspace binding; worktree provenance; explicit cleanup semantics and no-auto-merge rule. - Closure path: a first-party workflow child definition selects isolated worktree policy → Neo resolves and creates the dedicated worktree before child start → the child model/tool loop runs against that isolated workspace root → subsequent child file actions remain separated from other mutation cells → later merge/retirement decisions operate on preserved isolated results. - Boundary reachability: worktree is an explicit first-party neo.delegate/workflow child policy, and large-refactor is a shipped built-in ordinary workflow registry definition reachable through the standard Workflow tool. User/project workflows can use the same host API without inventing a separate isolation mechanism. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model&#39;s task discretion leaves the same definition-selected isolation relation available and enforced. The positive path is therefore constructor-owned C rather than autonomous A. - Evidence: lineage.rs; worktree.rs; large-refactor.lua; large-refactor.workflow.toml. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary DelegateSwarm children default to a shared workspace, so the positive state is intentionally limited to the first-party workflow constructor path that explicitly selects isolated worktrees.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: maintain a current whole-fleet view of active delegated/workflow operations and regulate present commitments through live guidance, resume/reallocation, pause/resume and interruption/cancellation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: several delegate agents, swarms and workflows can be queued, running, completed, failed, cancelled or timed out; current progress can make a commitment stale, blocked, misdirected or no longer worth consuming capacity. - Whole-system current view: the model-facing ListDelegates path exposes point-in-time agent/swarm state and aggregate queued/running/completed/failed/cancelled/timed-out counts; TaskList/TaskOutput expose workflow/background state. The operator /tasks browser projects the same current task/workflow population with current phase/children/progress. - Current-control decision scope: create/resume delegated capacity, inspect active workers, send live corrective messages, interrupt an agent or whole swarm, and in the parent mode pause/resume/stop workflows or answer a workflow&#39;s pending current-control question from the task browser. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide, from the current fleet state, which active commitments should continue unchanged, receive new guidance, resume, pause or terminate. - Decision owner: Base A mode — the main model-backed Neo agent through ListDelegates, MessageDelegate, InterruptDelegate, Delegate resume and task/result surfaces. Parent P mode — the human operator through the first-party /tasks task browser for workflow pause/resume/stop and pending workflow answers. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MultiAgentRuntime lifecycle registry, swarm aggregates, background-task manager, cancellation tokens, TaskList/TaskOutput, workflow operator projections, task-browser rendering/refresh and actor-tagged pause/resume/stop methods. - Closure path: current agent/swarm/workflow state is projected to the current-control owner → owner chooses guidance/resume/pause/stop/cancel action → Neo runtime applies the intervention to active commitments → subsequent child/workflow operation proceeds under the changed state and is visible in later snapshots. - Boundary reachability: all base control tools are shipped in the ordinary model-facing runtime. /tasks is a shipped interactive surface that calls BackgroundTaskManager pauseworkflow, resumeworkflow and stopwithactor with WorkflowActor::Human; no adopter-written supervisor is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: lifecycle registries and cancellation tokens only expose/enforce decisions. In the autonomous mode, removing the main model removes the semantic choice of which current worker to redirect, resume or interrupt. In the parent mode, the operator explicitly owns the same class of current-control intervention through the task browser. - Evidence: delegatecontrols.rs; delegate.rs; backgroundtasks.rs; interactive/input.rs; taskbrowser.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: hard concurrency/resource ceilings and scheduler state are treated as enforcement, not autonomous ownership. The parent-mode claim relies on the whole-task/workflow task browser plus actor-tagged control path, not on a generic emergency kill switch in isolation.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge implementation claims through separate read-only review agents that inspect repository reality across multiple review domains and return structured findings into later current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementing coding agent can report completion while code contains correctness, security, maintainability or test-coverage defects that its own operational path missed. - Claim being audited: the reviewed code/scope is correct and sufficiently safe/maintainable/tested, rather than merely that the implementing S1 reported success. - Ordinary reporting path: implementation results and any tests/checks selected by the implementing main/coder agent return through its normal model/tool or delegate result path. - Complementary access path: the shipped code-review workflow dispatches separate security, correctness and maintainability/test-gap agents with read-only tool ceilings, then dispatches a further reviewer to challenge weak findings; those agents inspect repository files through their own child contexts and return structured findings. - Independence boundary: review children are separately instantiated model-backed agents with dedicated Reviewer/Explorer profiles and no Write/Edit/Bash/Terminal mutation tools in the credited workflow. Their evidence path is distinct from the implementation agent&#39;s own transcript and self-verification, while still using the same project workspace. - Who acts on findings: the main model-backed agent that launched/owns the workflow receives the terminal workflow notification in the same durable session, can retrieve the canonical result with TaskOutput, and can then modify code, redirect/resume delegates or launch corrective work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: make the semantic audit judgment about security/correctness/maintainability/test gaps from complementary read-only repository evidence. - Decision owner: the model-backed review/challenge child agents; deterministic workflow code aggregates and deduplicates their structured output without owning the semantic review judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in code-review workflow and manifest; Reviewer/Explorer role profiles; read-only tool ceilings; workflow child schemas; durable workflow journal/result projection; notification queue and TaskOutput. - Closure path: main agent invokes shipped code-review → independent read-only reviewer agents inspect the target scope and produce findings → workflow aggregates a terminal structured result → same-session terminal notification directs the main agent to TaskOutput → findings re-enter current control and can cause corrective coding/delegation before later acceptance. - Boundary reachability: code-review is a shipped built-in ordinary workflow registry definition reachable through the standard model-facing Workflow runsaved path. It uses the same public Lua host API as other supported workflows and requires no adopter-authored reviewer implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the reviewer models leaves workflow schemas and deterministic aggregation but removes the semantic judgment about whether arbitrary code contains the cited defects. The positive state relies on separate reviewer contexts/read-only access and returned findings, not the name code-review by itself. - Evidence: code-review.lua; code-review.workflow.toml; builtins/mod.rs; workflow.rs; queue.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: review is an optional agent/workflow-chosen path rather than a mandatory acceptance gate for every code change. Workflow completion notification does not embed the findings themselves; it durably returns the result handle and canonical TaskOutput path for the next model turn.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then loop was established that senses external/future-relevant change, develops adaptation options and returns a selected option into Neo&#39;s present organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Neo exposes research, saved workflows, skills, history/session state, goals/plans, provider/tool integrations and a self-evo skill, but those mechanisms either support the current task, preserve history or require an explicit user-selected evidence scope rather than autonomously model a changing future environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for a qualifying prospective organizational adaptation function. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in deep-research workflow; self-evo skill; CreateSkill and skill-store reload; Workflow save/run surfaces; goal/plan state; MCP/provider configuration; durable sessions and context compaction. - Closure path: not applicable; no external/future distinction → adaptation option → persistent capability change → return into present S3/capability loop was found in the supported standard distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: self-evo explicitly distills an operator-selected history scope into reusable skills, while deep-research answers a current question using bounded local read-only children. Saved workflows/skills can persist capability, but the inspected runtime does not supply the prospective environmental sensing and adaptation decision loop that would make those writes S4. - Evidence: self-evo.md; deep-research.lua; skillsmanager.rs; workflow.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can intentionally use Neo&#39;s composable workflows/skills to build an adaptation process, but generic composability or current-task research does not donate S4 to the frozen standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A(P) - Function: establish and preserve the durable project-level identity, principles and standing operating instructions that govern later Neo coding agents in the workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missing, stale or inconsistent project identity and durable working principles can cause later agents to make incompatible assumptions about architecture, documentation, boundaries, conventions and what project rules should remain stable. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what project identity, durable principles, architecture assumptions, working practices and standing boundaries should govern later AI collaborators at the selected project recursion. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Base A — the model-backed main agent executing the shipped /init workflow synthesizes and writes the authoritative project AGENTS.md from repository evidence and user-supplied context. Parent P — the legitimate project owner can directly author/edit the same AGENTS.md authority, and /init guidance explicitly treats durable project principles as requiring explicit user approval to change. - Return-to-operation path: AGENTS.md persists in the project; the instruction resolver discovers applicable root/nested scopes without caching prior absence, produces instruction epochs, and the runtime injects/re-pins the resulting authority into subsequent model requests, including after compaction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the semantic content of the durable project AGENTS.md layer that later Neo model turns receive as standing project authority. - Decision owner: Base A mode — the main model-backed agent during shipped /init authoring/repair. Parent P mode — the legitimate project owner/editor when directly deciding or changing the same project-level instruction layer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /init system-reminder workflow; AGENTS.md structure validation/repair; instruction resolver and scope discovery; content-addressed instruction bundles; instruction epochs; preflight/reconciliation after tool execution; rehydration after compaction. - Closure path: project identity/policy gap or change → model-driven /init synthesis or legitimate owner edit → root AGENTS.md persists → instruction resolver observes the authoritative revision → instruction epoch enters the session/model context → later coding work is governed by the returned standing layer. - Boundary reachability: /init is a shipped interactive Neo surface that starts a normal model turn and repairs the resulting root AGENTS.md when needed. The ordinary instruction registry/resolver consumes AGENTS.md on supported project turns, and newly created instruction files are discovered before later tool/model batches. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: triggering /init does not predetermine the file&#39;s project-specific semantic content; the main model researches the workspace and chooses the synthesized identity/principles within higher-level user/Neo constraints. In the parent mode, the project owner directly owns the authoritative edit. Deterministic validation and instruction injection only enforce/transport the selected policy. - Evidence: initcommand.rs; interactive/mod.rs; resolver.rs; instructioncontext.rs; turnloop.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is project-recursion S5. It does not claim the model can rewrite Neo&#39;s higher-level application safety model, permission semantics or operator authority.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5386eedf89123db96cc30ee1d51bdad8feb77cc4/assessments/neo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5386eedf89123db96cc30ee1d51bdad8feb77cc4\">5386eed &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
